\id 2KI - Berean Standard Bible
\h 2 Kings
\toc2 2 Kings
\toc1 2 Kings
\mt1 2 Kings
\c 1
\s1 Elijah Denounces Ahaziah
\r (1 Kings 22:51–53)
\m
\v 1 After the death of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, Moab rebelled against Israel.
\b
\m
\v 2 Now \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313213"\x-wd* had fallen through the lattice of his upper room in Samaria and injured himself. So he sent messengers and instructed them: “Go inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron, whether I will recover from this injury.”
\b
\m
\v 3 But the angel of the LORD said to \x-wd Elijah the Tishbite|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, “Go up to meet the messengers of \x-wd the king of Samaria|id="Q313213"\x-wd* and ask them, ‘Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are on your way to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron?’
\v 4 Therefore this is what the LORD says: ‘You will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.’”
\b
\m So \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* departed.
\b
\m
\v 5 When the messengers returned to \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd*, he asked them, “Why have you returned?”
\b
\m
\v 6 They replied, “A man came up to meet us and said, ‘Go back to the king who sent you and tell him that this is what the LORD says: Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are sending these men to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.’”
\b
\m
\v 7 \x-wd The king|id="Q313213"\x-wd* asked them, “What sort of man came up to meet you and spoke these words to you?”
\b
\m
\v 8 “He was a hairy man,\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Or He had a garment of hair\f*” they answered, “with a leather belt around his waist.”
\b
\m “It was \x-wd Elijah the Tishbite|id="Q133507"\x-wd*,” said \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd*.
\b
\m
\v 9 Then \x-wd King Ahaziah|id="Q313213"\x-wd* sent to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* a captain with his company of fifty men. So the captain went up to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, who was sitting on top of a hill, and said to him, “Man of God, \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd* declares, ‘Come down!’”
\b
\m
\v 10 \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* answered the captain, “If I am a man of God, may fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty men.”
\b
\m And fire came down from heaven and consumed the captain and his fifty men.
\b
\m
\v 11 So \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd* sent to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* another captain with his fifty men. And the captain said to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, “Man of God, \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd* declares, ‘Come down at once!’”
\b
\m
\v 12 Again \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* replied, “If I am a man of God, may fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty men.”
\b
\m And the fire of God came down from heaven and consumed the captain and his fifty men.
\b
\m
\v 13 So \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd* sent a third captain with his fifty men. And the third captain went up, fell on his knees before \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, and begged him, “Man of God, may my life and the lives of these fifty servants of yours please be precious in your sight.
\v 14 Behold, fire has come down from heaven and consumed the first two captains of fifty, with all their men. But now may my life be precious in your sight.”
\b
\m
\v 15 Then the angel of the LORD said to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, “Go down with him. Do not be afraid of him.”
\b
\m So \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* got up and went down with him to \x-wd the king|id="Q313213"\x-wd*.
\b
\m
\v 16 And \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* said to \x-wd King Ahaziah|id="Q313213"\x-wd*, “This is what the LORD says: Is there really no God in Israel for you to inquire of His word? Is that why you have sent messengers to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.”
\s1 Jehoram Succeeds Ahaziah
\b
\m
\v 17 So \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313213"\x-wd* died according to the word of the LORD that \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* had spoken. And since he had no son, \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* \f + \fr 1:17 \ft Jehoram is a variant spelling of Joram.\f* succeeded him in the second year of the reign of \x-wd Jehoram son of \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd*|id="Q313215"\x-wd* over Judah.
\b
\m
\v 18 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313213"\x-wd*, along with his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel?
\c 2
\s1 Elijah Taken Up to Heaven
\m 
\v 1 Shortly before the LORD took \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* up to heaven in a whirlwind, \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* were on their way from Gilgal, 
\v 2 and \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Please stay here, for the LORD has sent me on to Bethel.” 
\b
\m But \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied, “As surely as the LORD lives and as you yourself live, I will not leave you.” 
\b
\m So they went down to Bethel. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then the sons of the prophets at Bethel came out to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and said, “Do you know that the LORD will take your master away from you today?” 
\b
\m “Yes, I know,” he replied. “Do not speak of it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Please stay here, for the LORD has sent me on to Jericho.” 
\b
\m But \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied, “As surely as the LORD lives and as you yourself live, I will not leave you.” 
\b
\m So they went to Jericho. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then the sons of the prophets at Jericho came up to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and said, “Do you know that the LORD will take your master away from you today?” 
\b
\m “Yes, I know,” he replied. “Do not speak of it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 And \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Please stay here, for the LORD has sent me on to the Jordan.” 
\b
\m But \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied, “As surely as the LORD lives and as you yourself live, I will not leave you.” 
\b
\m So the two of them went on. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then a company of fifty of the sons of the prophets went and stood at a distance, facing \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* as \x-wd the two of them|id="Q133507,Q206238"\x-wd* stood by the Jordan. 
\v 8 And \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* took his cloak, rolled it up, and struck the waters, which parted to the right and to the left, so that the two of them crossed over on dry ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 After they had crossed over, \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Tell me, what can I do for you before I am taken away from you?” 
\b
\m “Please, let me inherit a double portion of your spirit,” \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “You have requested a difficult thing,” said \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*. “Nevertheless, if you see me as I am taken from you, it will be yours. But if not, then it will not be so.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 As they were walking along and talking together, suddenly a chariot of fire with horses of fire appeared and separated the two of them, and \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* went up into heaven in a whirlwind. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 As \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* watched, he cried out, “My father, my father, the chariots and horsemen of Israel!” And he saw \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* no more. So taking hold of his own clothes, he tore them in two. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* also picked up the cloak that had fallen from \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*, and he went back and stood on the bank of the Jordan. 
\v 14 Then he took the cloak of \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* that had fallen from him and struck the waters. “Where now is the LORD, the God of \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*?” he asked. 
\b
\m And when he had struck the waters, they parted to the right and to the left, and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* crossed over. 
\s1 Elisha Succeeds Elijah
\b
\m 
\v 15 When the sons of the prophets who were watching him from Jericho saw what had happened, they said, “The spirit of \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd* rests on \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*.” And they went to meet him and bowed down to the ground before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “Look now,” they said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “we your servants have fifty valiant men. Please let them go and search for \x-wd your master|id="Q133507"\x-wd*. Perhaps the Spirit of the LORD has taken him up and put him on one of the mountains or in one of the valleys.” 
\b
\m “Do not send them,” \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 But when they pressed him to the point of embarrassment, he said, “Send them.” 
\b
\m And they sent fifty men, who searched for three days but did not find \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 When they returned to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, who was staying in Jericho, he said to them, “Didn’t I tell you not to go?” 
\s1 Elisha Heals the Waters of Jericho
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then the men of the city said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Please note, \x-wd our lord|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, that the city’s location is good, as you can see. But the water is bad and the land is unfruitful.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 “Bring me a new bowl,” he replied, “and put some salt in it.” 
\b
\m So they brought it to him, 
\v 21 and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went out to the spring, cast the salt into it, and said, “This is what the LORD says: ‘I have healed this water. No longer will it cause death or unfruitfulness.\f + \fr 2:21 \ft Or barrenness\f*’” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And the waters there have been healthy to this day, according to the word spoken by \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Elisha Mocked
\b
\m 
\v 23 From there, \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went up to Bethel, and as he was walking up the road, a group of boys \f + \fr 2:23 \ft Or some small youths or some insignificant young men; similarly in verse 24\f* came out of the city and jeered at him, chanting, “Go up, \x-wd you baldhead|id="Q206238"\x-wd*! Go up, \x-wd you baldhead|id="Q206238"\x-wd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then he turned around, looked at them, and called down a curse on them in the name of the LORD. 
\b
\m Suddenly two female bears came out of the woods and mauled forty-two of the boys. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went on to Mount Carmel, and from there he returned to Samaria. 
\c 3
\s1 Moab’s Rebellion
\m 
\v 1 In the eighteenth year of \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd*’s reign over Judah, \x-wd Jehoram \f + \fr 3:1 \ft Jehoram is a variant spelling of Joram; also in verse 6.\f* son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria twelve years. 
\v 2 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, but not as \x-wd his father|id="Q235901"\x-wd* and \x-wd mother|id="Q721295"\x-wd* had done. He removed the sacred pillar of Baal that \x-wd his father|id="Q235901"\x-wd* had made. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Nevertheless, he clung to the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit; he did not turn away from them. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now \x-wd Mesha king of Moab|id="Q350258"\x-wd* was a sheep breeder,\f + \fr 3:4 \ft Or sheepherder\f* and he would render to the king of Israel a hundred thousand lambs and the wool of a hundred thousand rams. 
\v 5 But after the death of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, \x-wd the king of Moab|id="Q350258"\x-wd* rebelled against \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd*. 
\v 6 So at that time \x-wd King Jehoram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* set out from Samaria and mobilized all Israel. 
\v 7 And he sent a message to \x-wd Jehoshaphat king of Judah|id="Q126689"\x-wd*: “\x-wd The king of Moab|id="Q350258"\x-wd* has rebelled against me. Will you go with me to fight against Moab?” 
\b
\m “I will go,” replied \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd*. “I am as you are, my people are your people, and my horses are your horses.” 
\v 8 Then he asked, “Which way shall we go up?” 
\b
\m “By way of the Desert of Edom,” replied \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So the \x-wd king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, \x-wd the king of Judah|id="Q126689"\x-wd*, and \x-wd the king of Edom|id=""\x-wd* set out, and after they had traveled a roundabout route for seven days, they had no water for their army or for their animals. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “Alas,” said \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, “for the LORD has summoned \x-wd these three kings|id="Q334739,Q126689"\x-wd* to deliver them into the hand of Moab!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 But \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd* asked, “Is there no prophet of the LORD here? Let us inquire of the LORD through him.” 
\b
\m And one of the servants of \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd* answered, “\x-wd Elisha son of \x-wd Shaphat|id="Q114685996"\x-wd*|id="Q206238"\x-wd* is here. He used to pour water on the hands of \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Or He was Elijah’s personal assistant\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd* affirmed, “The word of the LORD is with him.” So \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd* and \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q126689"\x-wd* and the king of Edom went down to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, however, said to \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, “What have we to do with each other? Go to the prophets of your father and of your mother!” 
\b
\m “No,” replied \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, “for it is the LORD who has summoned \x-wd these three kings|id="Q334739,Q126689"\x-wd* to deliver them into the hand of Moab.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “As surely as the LORD of Hosts lives, before whom I stand, were it not for my regard for the presence of \x-wd Jehoshaphat king of Judah|id="Q126689"\x-wd*, I would not look at you or acknowledge you. 
\v 15 But now, bring me a harpist.\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Or a musician; twice in this verse\f*” 
\b
\m And while the harpist played, the hand of the LORD came upon \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* 
\v 16 and he said, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Dig this valley full of ditches.’ 
\v 17 For the LORD says, ‘You will not see wind or rain, but the valley will be filled with water, and you will drink—you and your cattle and your animals.’ 
\v 18 This is a simple matter in the sight of the LORD, and He will also deliver the Moabites into your hand. 
\v 19 And you shall attack every fortified city and every city of importance. You shall cut down every good tree, stop up every spring, and ruin every good field with stones.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 The next morning, at the time of the morning sacrifice, water suddenly flowed from the direction of Edom and filled the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Now all the Moabites had heard that \x-wd the kings|id="Q334739,Q126689"\x-wd* had come up to fight against them. So all who could bear arms, young and old, were summoned and stationed at the border. 
\v 22 When they got up early in the morning, the sun was shining on the water, and it looked as red as blood to the Moabites across the way. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “This is blood!” they exclaimed. “\x-wd The kings|id="Q334739,Q126689"\x-wd* have clashed swords and slaughtered one another. Now to the plunder, Moab!” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 But when the Moabites came to the camp of Israel, the Israelites rose up and attacked them, and they fled before them. So the Israelites invaded their land and struck down the Moabites. 
\v 25 They destroyed the cities, and each man threw stones on every good field until it was covered. They stopped up every spring and cut down every good tree. Only Kir-haraseth was left with stones in place, but men with slings surrounded it and attacked it as well. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 When \x-wd the king of Moab|id="Q350258"\x-wd* saw that the battle was too fierce for him, he took with him seven hundred swordsmen to break through to the king of Edom, but they could not prevail. 
\v 27 So he took his firstborn son, who was to succeed him, and offered him as a burnt offering on the city wall. 
\b
\m And there was great fury against the Israelites,\f + \fr 3:27 \ft Or And Israel’s fury was great\f* so they withdrew and returned to their own land. 
\c 4
\s1 The Widow’s Oil
\m 
\v 1 Now the wife of one of the sons of the prophets cried out to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Your servant, my husband, is dead, and you know that your servant feared the LORD. And now his creditor is coming to take my two children as his slaves!” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “How can I help you?” asked \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. “Tell me, what do you have in the house?” 
\b
\m She answered, “Your servant has nothing in the house but a jar of oil.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “Go,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “borrow empty jars from all your neighbors. Do not gather just a few. 
\v 4 Then go inside, shut the door behind you and your sons, and pour oil into all these jars, setting the full ones aside.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So she left him, and after she had shut the door behind her and her sons, they kept bringing jars to her, and she kept pouring. 
\v 6 When all the jars were full, she said to her son, “Bring me another.” 
\b
\m But he replied, “There are no more jars.” Then the oil stopped flowing. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 She went and told \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, and he said, “Go, sell the oil, and pay your debt. Then you and your sons can live on the remainder.” 
\s1 The Shunammite Woman
\r (Matthew 10:40–42)
\b
\m 
\v 8 One day \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went to Shunem, and \x-wd a prominent woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd* who lived there persuaded him to have a meal. So whenever he would pass by, he would stop there to eat. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \x-wd the woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd* said to her husband, “Behold, now I know that the one who often comes our way is a holy man of God. 
\v 10 Please let us make a small room upstairs and put in it a bed, a table, a chair, and a lamp for him. Then when he comes to us, he can stay there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 One day \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* came to visit and went to his upper room to lie down. 
\v 12 And he said to \x-wd Gehazi his servant|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Call \x-wd the Shunammite woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m And when he had called her, she stood before him, 
\v 13 and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Now tell her, ‘Look, you have gone to all this trouble for us. What can we do for you? Can we speak on your behalf to the king or the commander of the army?’” 
\b
\m “I have a home among my own people,” she replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So he asked, “Then what should be done for her?” 
\b
\m “Well, she has no son,” \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied, “and her husband is old.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “Call her,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m So \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* called her, and she stood in the doorway. 
\v 16 And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* declared, “At this time next year, you will hold a son in your arms.” 
\b
\m “No, my lord,” she said. “Do not lie to \x-wd your maidservant|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*, O \x-wd man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 But \x-wd the woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd* did conceive, and at that time the next year she gave birth to a son, just as \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had told her. 
\s1 Elisha Raises the Shunammite’s Son
\r (Acts 20:7–12)
\b
\m 
\v 18 And the child grew, and one day he went out to his father, who was with the harvesters. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 “My head! My head!” he complained to his father. 
\b
\m So his father told a servant, “Carry him to \x-wd his mother|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 After the servant had picked him up and carried him to \x-wd his mother|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*, the boy sat on her lap until noon, and then he died. 
\v 21 And she went up and laid him on the bed of \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. Then she shut the door and went out. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \x-wd the woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd* called her husband and said, “Please send me one of the servants and one of the donkeys, that I may go quickly to \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and return.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “Why would you go to him today?” he replied. “It is not a New Moon or a Sabbath.” 
\b
\m “Everything is all right,” she said. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then she saddled the donkey and told her servant, “Drive onward; do not slow the pace for me unless I tell you.” 
\v 25 So she set out and went to \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* at Mount Carmel. 
\b
\m When \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* saw her at a distance, he said to \x-wd his servant Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Look, there is \x-wd the Shunammite woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*. 
\v 26 Please run out now to meet her and ask, ‘Are you all right? Is your husband all right? Is your child all right?’” 
\b
\m And she answered, “Everything is all right.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When she reached \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* at the mountain, she clung to his feet. \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* came over to push her away, but \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Leave her alone, for her soul is in deep distress, and the LORD has hidden it from me and has not told me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then she said, “Did I ask you for a son, my lord? Didn’t I say, ‘Do not deceive me?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Tie up your garment,\f + \fr 4:29 \ft Literally Gird up your loins\f* take my staff in your hand, and go! If you meet anyone, do not greet him, and if anyone greets you, do not answer him. Then lay my staff on the boy’s face.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 And \x-wd the mother of the boy|id="Q4428768"\x-wd* said, “As surely as the LORD lives and as you yourself live, I will not leave you.” So he got up and followed her. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went on ahead of them and laid the staff on the boy’s face, but there was no sound or response. So he went back to meet \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and told him, “The boy has not awakened.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 When \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* reached the house, there was the boy lying dead on his bed. 
\v 33 So he went in, closed the door behind the two of them, and prayed to the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* got on the bed and lay on the boy, mouth to mouth, eye to eye, and hand to hand. As he stretched himself out over him, the boy’s body became warm. 
\v 35 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* turned away and paced back and forth across the room. Then he got on the bed and stretched himself out over the boy again, and the boy sneezed seven times and opened his eyes. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* summoned \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and said, “Call \x-wd the Shunammite woman|id="Q4428768"\x-wd*.” So he called her and she came. 
\b
\m Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Pick up your son.” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 She came in, fell at his feet, and bowed to the ground. Then she picked up her son and went out. 
\s1 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* Purifies the Poisonous Stew
\b
\m 
\v 38 When \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* returned to Gilgal, there was a famine in the land. As the sons of the prophets were sitting at his feet, he said to his attendant, “Put on the large pot and boil some stew for the sons of the prophets.” 
\b
\m 
\v 39 One of them went out to the field to gather herbs, and he found a wild vine from which he gathered as many wild gourds as his garment could hold. Then he came back and cut them up into the pot of stew, though no one knew what they were. 
\b
\m 
\v 40 And they poured it out for the men to eat, but when they tasted the stew they cried out, “There is death in the pot, O man of God!” And they could not eat it. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Get some flour.” He threw it into the pot and said, “Pour it out for the people to eat.” And there was nothing harmful in the pot. 
\s1 Feeding a Hundred Men
\r (Matthew 15:29–39; Mark 8:1–10)
\b
\m 
\v 42 Now a man from Baal-shalishah came to \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* with a sack of twenty loaves of barley bread from the first ripe grain. 
\b
\m “Give it to the people to eat,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 But his servant asked, “How am I to set twenty loaves before a hundred men?” 
\b
\m “Give it to the people to eat,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “for this is what the LORD says: ‘They will eat and have some left over.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 44 So he set it before them, and they ate and had some left over, according to the word of the LORD. 
\c 5
\s1 Naaman Cured of Leprosy
\r (Luke 17:11–19)
\m 
\v 1 Now \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*, the commander of the army of \x-wd the king of Aram|id=""\x-wd*, was a great man in \x-wd his master|id=""\x-wd*’s sight and highly regarded, for through him the LORD had given victory to Aram. And he was a mighty man of valor, but he was a leper.\f + \fr 5:1 \ft A leper, or one with leprosy, was one afflicted with a skin disease; here and throughout this chapter; see Leviticus 13.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 2 At this time the Arameans had gone out in bands and had taken a young girl from the land of Israel, and she was serving \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*’s wife. 
\v 3 She said to her mistress, “If only \x-wd my master|id=""\x-wd* would go to \x-wd the prophet who is in Samaria|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, he would cure him of his leprosy.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* went and told \x-wd his master|id=""\x-wd* what the girl from the land of Israel had said. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 “Go now,” said \x-wd the king of Aram|id=""\x-wd*, “and I will send you with a letter to the king of Israel.” 
\b
\m So \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* departed, taking with him ten talents of silver,\f + \fr 5:5 \ft 10 talents is approximately 754 pounds or 342 kilograms of silver.\f* six thousand shekels of gold,\f + \fr 5:5 \ft 6,000 shekels is approximately 150.8 pounds or 68.4 kilograms of gold.\f* and ten sets of clothing. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 And the letter that he took to the king of Israel stated: “With this letter I am sending my servant \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*, so that you may cure him of his leprosy.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 When the king of Israel read the letter, he tore his clothes and asked, “Am I God, killing and giving life, that this man expects me to cure a leper? Surely you can see that he is seeking a quarrel with me!” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now when \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* the man of God heard that the king of Israel had torn his clothes, he sent a message to the king: “Why have you torn your clothes? Please let \x-wd the man|id="Q126778"\x-wd* come to me, and he will know that there is a prophet in Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* came with his horses and chariots and stood at the door of \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*’s house. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* sent him a messenger, who said, “Go and wash yourself seven times in the Jordan, and your flesh will be restored, and you will be clean.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 But \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* went away angry, saying, “I thought that he would surely come out, stand and call on the name of the LORD his God, and wave his hand over the spot to cure my leprosy. 
\v 12 Are not the Abanah and Pharpar, the rivers of Damascus, better than all the waters of Israel? Could I not have washed in them and been cleansed?” So he turned and went away in a rage. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*’s servants, however, approached him and said, “My father, if \x-wd the prophet|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had told you to do some great thing, would you not have done it? How much more, then, when he tells you, ‘Wash and be cleansed’?” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* went down and dipped himself in the Jordan seven times, according to the word of \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, and his flesh was restored and became like that of a little child, and he was clean. 
\s1 Gehazi’s Greed and Leprosy
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* and all his attendants went back to \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, stood before him, and declared, “Now I know for sure that there is no God in all the earth except in Israel. So please accept a gift from \x-wd your servant|id="Q126778"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 But \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied, “As surely as the LORD lives, before whom I stand, I will not accept it.” And although \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* urged him to accept it, he refused. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “If you will not,” said \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*, “please let me, \x-wd your servant|id="Q126778"\x-wd*, be given as much soil as a pair of mules can carry. For \x-wd your servant|id="Q126778"\x-wd* will never again make a burnt offering or a sacrifice to any other god but the LORD. 
\v 18 Yet may the LORD forgive \x-wd your servant|id="Q126778"\x-wd* this one thing: When my master goes into the temple of Rimmon to worship there, and he leans on my arm, and I bow down in the temple of Rimmon, may the LORD forgive \x-wd your servant|id="Q126778"\x-wd* in this matter.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 “Go in peace,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m But after \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* had traveled a short distance, 
\v 20 \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, the servant of \x-wd Elisha the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, said, “Look, \x-wd my master|id="Q206238"\x-wd* has spared this Aramean, \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*, by not accepting what he brought. As surely as the LORD lives, I will run after him and get something from him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* pursued \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd*. And when \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* saw him running toward him, he got down from the chariot to meet him and asked, “Is everything all right?” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “Everything is all right,” \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied. “\x-wd My master|id="Q206238"\x-wd* has sent me to say, ‘Look, two young men from the sons of the prophets have just now come to me from the hill country of Ephraim. Please give them a talent of silver \f + \fr 5:22 \ft A talent is approximately 75.4 pounds or 34.2 kilograms of silver.\f* and two sets of clothing.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 But \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* insisted, “Please, take two talents.” And he urged \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* to accept them. Then he tied up two talents of silver in two bags along with two sets of clothing and gave them to two of his servants, who carried them ahead of \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 When \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* came to the hill, he took the gifts from the servants and stored them in the house. Then he dismissed the men, and they departed. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 When \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went in and stood before \x-wd his master|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* asked him, “\x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, where have you been?” 
\b
\m “\x-wd Your servant|id="Q206238"\x-wd* did not go anywhere,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 But \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* questioned him, “Did not my spirit go with you when the man got down from his chariot to meet you? Is this the time to accept money and clothing, olive groves and vineyards, sheep and oxen, menservants and maidservants? 
\v 27 Therefore, the leprosy of \x-wd Naaman|id="Q126778"\x-wd* will cling to you and your descendants forever!” 
\b
\m And as \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* left his presence, he was leprous—as white as snow. 
\c 6
\s1 The Axe Head Floats
\m 
\v 1 Now the sons of the prophets said to \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Please take note that the place where we meet with you is too small for us. 
\v 2 Please let us go to the Jordan, where each of us can get a log so we can build ourselves a place to live there.” 
\b
\m “Go,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then one of them said, “Please come with your servants.” 
\b
\m “I will come,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* went with them, and when they came to the Jordan, they began to cut down some trees. 
\v 5 As one of them was cutting down a tree, the iron axe head fell into the water. “Oh, \x-wd my master|id="Q206238"\x-wd*,” he cried out, “it was borrowed!” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 “Where did it fall?” asked \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m And when he showed him the place, \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* cut a stick, threw it there, and made the iron float. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 “Lift it out,” he said, and the man reached out his hand and took it. 
\s1 Elisha Captures the Blinded Arameans
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now \x-wd the king of Aram|id=""\x-wd* was at war against Israel. After consulting with his servants, he said, “My camp will be in such and such a place.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* sent word to \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd*: “Be careful not to pass by this place, for the Arameans are going down there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd* sent word to the place \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had pointed out. Time and again \f + \fr 6:10 \ft Literally Not once and not twice\f* \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* warned \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*, so that he was on his guard in such places. 
\v 11 For this reason \x-wd the king of Aram|id=""\x-wd* became enraged and called his servants to demand of them, “Tell me, which one of us is on the side of \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 But one of his servants replied, “No one, \x-wd my lord the king|id=""\x-wd*. For \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, the prophet in Israel, tells \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd* the very words you speak in your bedroom.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* said, “Go and see where he is, that I may send men to capture him.” 
\b
\m On receiving the report, “\x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* is in Dothan,” 
\v 14 \x-wd the king of Aram|id=""\x-wd* sent horses, chariots, and a great army. They went there by night and surrounded the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When the servant of \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* got up and went out early in the morning, behold, an army with horses and chariots had surrounded the city. So he asked \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Oh, \x-wd my master|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, what are we to do?” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “Do not be afraid,” \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* answered, “for those who are with us are more than those who are with them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* prayed, “O LORD, please open his eyes that he may see.” 
\b
\m And the LORD opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw that the hills were full of horses and chariots of fire all around \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 As the Arameans came down against him, \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* prayed to the LORD, “Please strike these people with blindness.” So He struck them with blindness, according to the word of \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* told them, “This is not the way, and this is not the city. Follow me, and I will take you to the man you are seeking.” And he led them to Samaria. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 When they had entered Samaria, \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “O LORD, open the eyes of these men that they may see.” 
\b
\m Then the LORD opened their eyes, and they looked around and discovered that they were in Samaria. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 And when \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd* saw them, he asked \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “My father, shall I kill them? Shall I kill them?” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “Do not kill them,” he replied. “Would you kill those you have captured with your own sword or bow? Set food and water before them, that they may eat and drink and then return to their master.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* prepared a great feast for them, and after they had finished eating and drinking, he sent them away, and they returned to their master. And the Aramean raiders did not come into the land of Israel again. 
\s1 The Siege and Famine of Samaria
\b
\m 
\v 24 Some time later, \x-wd Ben-hadad king of Aram|id="Q717814"\x-wd* assembled his entire army and marched up to besiege Samaria. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 So there was a great famine in Samaria. Indeed, they besieged the city so long that a donkey’s head sold for eighty shekels of silver,\f + \fr 6:25 \ft 80 shekels is approximately 2 pounds or 907.2 grams of silver.\f* and a quarter cab of dove’s dung \f + \fr 6:25 \ft Or a quarter cab of seed pods; that is, approximately 0.28 dry quarts or 0.31 liters\f* sold for five shekels of silver.\f + \fr 6:25 \ft 5 shekels is approximately 2 ounces or 57 grams of silver.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 26 As \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd* was passing by on the wall, a woman cried out to him, “Help me, my lord \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 He answered, “If the LORD does not help you, where can I find help for you? From the threshing floor or the winepress?” 
\v 28 Then \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* asked her, “What is the matter?” 
\b
\m And she answered, “This woman said to me, ‘Give up your son, that we may eat him, and tomorrow we will eat my son.’ 
\v 29 So we boiled my son and ate him, and the next day I said to her, ‘Give up your son, that we may eat him.’ But she had hidden her son.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 When \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* heard the words of the woman, he tore his clothes. And as he passed by on the wall, the people saw the sackcloth under his clothes next to his skin. 
\v 31 He announced, “May God punish me, and ever so severely, if the head of \x-wd Elisha son of \x-wd Shaphat|id="Q114685996"\x-wd*|id="Q206238"\x-wd* remains on his shoulders through this day!” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 Now \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* was sitting in his house, and the elders were sitting with him. \x-wd The king|id=""\x-wd* sent a messenger ahead, but before he arrived, \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said to the elders, “Do you see how this murderer has sent someone to cut off my head? Look, when the messenger comes, shut the door to keep him out. Is not the sound of his master’s footsteps behind him?” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 While \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* was still speaking with them, the messenger came down to him. And \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* said, “This calamity is from the LORD. Why should I wait for the LORD any longer?” 
\c 7
\s1 Elisha’s Prophecy of Plenty
\m 
\v 1 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Hear the word of the LORD! This is what the LORD says: ‘About this time tomorrow at the gate of Samaria, a seah of fine flour \f + \fr 7:1 \ft A seah is approximately 6.6 dry quarts or 7.3 liters (probably about 8.2 pounds or 3.7 kilograms of flour); here and in verses 16 and 18.\f* will sell for a shekel,\f + \fr 7:1 \ft A shekel is approximately 0.4 ounces or 11.4 grams, probably of silver; here and in verses 16 and 18.\f* and two seahs of barley \f + \fr 7:1 \ft 2 seahs is approximately 13.2 dry quarts or 14.6 liters (probably about 19.3 pounds or 8.8 kilograms of barley); here and in verses 16 and 18.\f* will sell for a shekel.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 But the officer on whose arm \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* leaned answered \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Look, even if the LORD were to make windows in heaven, could this really happen?” 
\b
\m “You will see it with your own eyes,” replied \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “but you will not eat any of it.” 
\s1 The Syrians Flee
\b
\m 
\v 3 Now there were four men with leprosy \f + \fr 7:3 \ft Leprosy was a term used for various skin diseases; here and in verse 8; see Leviticus 13.\f* at the entrance of the city gate, and they said to one another, “Why just sit here until we die? 
\v 4 If we say, ‘Let us go into the city,’ we will die there from the famine in the city; but if we sit here, we will also die. So come now, let us go over to the camp of the Arameans. If they let us live, we will live; if they kill us, we will die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So they arose at twilight and went to the camp of the Arameans. But when they came to the outskirts of the camp, there was not a man to be found. 
\v 6 For the Lord had caused the Arameans to hear the sound of chariots, horses, and a great army, so that they said to one another, “Look, \x-wd the king of Israel|id=""\x-wd* must have hired the kings of the Hittites and Egyptians to attack us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Thus the Arameans had arisen and fled at twilight, abandoning their tents and horses and donkeys. The camp was intact, and they had run for their lives. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 When the lepers reached the edge of the camp, they went into a tent to eat and drink. Then they carried off the silver, gold, and clothing, and went and hid them. On returning, they entered another tent, carried off some items from there, and hid them. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Finally, they said to one another, “We are not doing what is right. Today is a day of good news. If we are silent and wait until morning light, our sin will overtake us. Now, therefore, let us go and tell \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*’s household.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So they went and called out to the gatekeepers of the city, saying, “We went to the Aramean camp and no one was there—not a trace—only tethered horses and donkeys, and the tents were intact.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 The gatekeepers shouted the news, and it was reported to \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*’s household. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* got up in the night and said to his servants, “Let me tell you what the Arameans have done to us. They know we are starving, so they have left the camp to hide in the field, thinking, ‘When they come out of the city, we will take them alive and enter the city.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 But one of his servants replied, “Please, have scouts take five of the horses that remain in the city. Their plight will be no worse than all the Israelites who are left here. You can see that all the Israelites here are doomed. So let us send them and find out.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then the scouts took two chariots with horses, and \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* sent them after the Aramean army, saying, “Go and see.” 
\v 15 And they tracked them as far as the Jordan, and indeed, the whole way was littered with the clothing and equipment the Arameans had thrown off in haste. So the scouts returned and told \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*. 
\s1 Elisha’s Prophecy Fulfilled
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then the people went out and plundered the camp of the Arameans. It was then that a seah of fine flour sold for a shekel, and two seahs of barley sold for a shekel, according to the word of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Now \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* had appointed the officer on whose arm he leaned to be in charge of the gate, but the people trampled him in the gateway, and he died, just as \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had foretold when \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* had come to him. 
\v 18 It happened just as \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had told \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*: “About this time tomorrow at the gate of Samaria, two seahs of barley will sell for a shekel, and a seah of fine flour will sell for a shekel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And the officer had answered \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Look, even if the LORD were to make windows in heaven, could this really happen?” 
\b
\m So \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had replied, “You will see it with your own eyes, but you will not eat any of it!” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And that is just what happened to him. The people trampled him in the gateway, and he died. 
\c 8
\s1 The Shunammite’s Land Restored
\m 
\v 1 Now \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had said to \x-wd the woman whose son he had restored to life|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, “Arise, you and your household; go and live as a foreigner wherever you can. For the LORD has decreed that a seven-year famine will come to the land.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \x-wd the woman|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had proceeded to do as \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had instructed. And she and her household lived as foreigners for seven years in the land of the Philistines. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 At the end of seven years, when \x-wd the woman|id="Q206238"\x-wd* returned from the land of the Philistines, she went to \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* to appeal for her house and her land. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* had been speaking to \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, the servant of \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, saying, “Please relate to me all the great things \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* has done.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* was telling \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* how \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had brought the dead back to life. Just then \x-wd the woman whose son \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had revived|id="Q206238"\x-wd* came to appeal to \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* for her house and her land. So \x-wd Gehazi|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “My lord \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*, this is \x-wd the woman|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, and this is the son \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* restored to life.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* asked \x-wd the woman|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, she confirmed it. So \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* appointed for her an officer, saying, “Restore all that was hers, along with all the proceeds of the field from the day that she left the country until now.” 
\s1 Hazael Murders Ben-hadad
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* came to Damascus while \x-wd Ben-hadad king of Aram|id="Q717814"\x-wd* was sick, and \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* was told, “\x-wd The man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* has come here.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* said to \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd*, “Take a gift in your hand, go to meet \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, and inquire of the LORD through him, ‘Will I recover from this illness?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* went to meet \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, taking with him a gift of forty camel loads of every good thing from Damascus. And he went in and stood before him and said, “Your son \x-wd Ben-hadad king of Aram|id="Q717814"\x-wd* has sent me to ask, ‘Will I recover from this illness?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* answered, “Go and tell him, ‘You will surely recover.’ But the LORD \f + \fr 8:10 \ft Or “Go and say, ‘You will surely not recover.’ And the LORD\f* has shown me that in fact he will die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* fixed his gaze steadily on him until \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* became uncomfortable. Then \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* began to weep. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “Why is my lord weeping?” asked \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m “Because I know the evil you will do to the Israelites,” \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* replied. “You will set fire to their fortresses, kill their young men with the sword, dash their little ones to pieces, and rip open their pregnant women.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “But how could your servant, a mere dog, do such a monstrous thing?” said \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* answered, “The LORD has shown me that you will be king over Aram.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* left \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* and went to \x-wd his master|id="Q717814"\x-wd*, who asked him, “What did \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* say to you?” 
\b
\m And he replied, “He told me that you would surely recover.” 
\v 15 But the next day \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* took a thick cloth, dipped it in water, and spread it over \x-wd the king|id="Q717814"\x-wd*’s face. 
\b
\m So \x-wd Ben-hadad|id="Q717814"\x-wd* died, and \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Jehoram Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 21:1–7)
\b
\m 
\v 16 In the fifth year of the reign of \x-wd Joram son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Jehoram son of \x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q206238"\x-wd*|id="Q313215"\x-wd* succeeded \x-wd his father|id="Q206238"\x-wd* as king of Judah. 
\v 17 \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd* was thirty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eight years. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 And \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd* walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, just as the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* had done. For he married \x-wd a daughter of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q463934"\x-wd* and did evil in the sight of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Yet for the sake of His servant \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*, the LORD was unwilling to destroy Judah, since He had promised to maintain a lamp for \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* and his descendants forever. 
\s1 Edom and Libnah Rebel 
\r (2 Chronicles 21:8–11)
\b
\m 
\v 20 In the days of \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd*,\f + \fr 8:20 \ft Literally In his days\f* Edom rebelled against the hand of Judah and appointed their own king. 
\v 21 So \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd* \f + \fr 8:21 \ft Hebrew Joram, a variant of Jehoram (the son of Jehoshaphat), as in verse 16; also in verses 23 and 24\f* crossed over to Zair with all his chariots. When the Edomites surrounded him and his chariot commanders, he rose up and attacked by night. His troops, however, fled to their homes. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So to this day Edom has been in rebellion against the hand of Judah. Likewise, Libnah rebelled at the same time. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 24 And \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*. And his son \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Ahaziah Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 22:1–7)
\b
\m 
\v 25 In the twelfth year of the reign of \x-wd Joram son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Ahaziah son of \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd*|id="Q313214"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 26 \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* was twenty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem one year. \x-wd His mother|id="Q463934"\x-wd*’s name was \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd*, the granddaughter of \x-wd Omri king of Israel|id="Q313221"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 And \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* walked in the ways of the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* and did evil in the sight of the LORD like the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, for he was a son-in-law of the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* went with \x-wd Joram son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd* to fight against \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* at Ramoth-gilead, and the Arameans wounded \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd*. 
\v 29 So \x-wd King Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* returned to Jezreel to recover from the wounds that the Arameans had inflicted on him at Ramah \f + \fr 8:29 \ft Ramah is a variant of Ramoth; see verse 28.\f* when he fought against \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd*. Then \x-wd Ahaziah son of \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd* king of Judah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* went down to Jezreel to visit \x-wd Joram son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, because \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* had been wounded. 
\c 9
\s1 Jehu Anointed King of Israel
\m 
\v 1 Now \x-wd Elisha the prophet|id="Q206238"\x-wd* summoned one of the sons of the prophets and said to him, “Tuck your cloak under your belt,\f + \fr 9:1 \ft Literally Gird up your loins\f* take this flask of oil, and go to Ramoth-gilead. 
\v 2 When you arrive, look for \x-wd Jehu son of Jehoshaphat|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, the son of Nimshi. Go in, get him away from his companions, and take him to an inner room. 
\v 3 Then take the flask of oil, pour it on his head, and declare, ‘This is what the LORD says: I anoint you king over Israel.’ Then open the door and run. Do not delay!” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So the young prophet went to Ramoth-gilead, 
\v 5 and when he arrived, the army commanders were sitting there. “I have a message for you, \x-wd commander|id="Q313225"\x-wd*,” he said. 
\b
\m “For which of us?” asked \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m “For you, \x-wd commander|id="Q313225"\x-wd*,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* got up and went into the house, where the young prophet poured the oil on his head and declared, “This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘I anoint you king over the LORD’s people Israel. 
\v 7 And you are to strike down the house of your master \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, so that I may avenge the blood of My servants the prophets and the blood of all the servants of the LORD shed by the hand of \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd*. 
\v 8 The whole house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* will perish, and I will cut off from \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* every male,\f + \fr 9:8 \ft Literally (all) those who urinate against a wall\f* both slave and free, in Israel. 
\v 9 I will make the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* like the houses of \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* and \x-wd Baasha son of \x-wd Ahijah|id="Q114841071"\x-wd*|id="Q313224"\x-wd*. 
\v 10 And on the plot of ground at Jezreel the dogs will devour \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd*, and there will be no one to bury her.’” 
\b
\m Then the young prophet opened the door and ran. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* went out to the servants of \x-wd his master|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, they asked, “Is everything all right? Why did this madman come to you?” 
\b
\m “You know his kind and their babble,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “That is a lie!” they said. “Tell us now!” 
\b
\m So \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* answered, “He talked to me about this and that, saying, ‘This is what the LORD says: I anoint you king over Israel.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Quickly, each man took his garment and put it under \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* on the bare steps. Then they blew the ram’s horn and proclaimed, “\x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* is king!” 
\s1 Jehu Kills Joram and Ahaziah
\r (2 Chronicles 22:8–9)
\b
\m 
\v 14 Thus \x-wd Jehu son of Jehoshaphat|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, the son of Nimshi, conspired against \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m (Now \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* and all Israel had been defending Ramoth-gilead against \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd*, 
\v 15 but \x-wd King Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* \f + \fr 9:15 \ft Or Jehoram, a variant of Joram; also in verses 17, 21, 22, 23, and 24\f* had returned to Jezreel to recover from the wounds he had suffered at the hands of the Arameans in the battle against \x-wd Hazael their king|id="Q127170"\x-wd*.) 
\b
\m So \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* said, “If you commanders wish to make me king, then do not let anyone escape from the city to go and tell it in Jezreel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* got into his chariot and went to Jezreel, because \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* was laid up there. And \x-wd Ahaziah king of Judah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* had gone down to see him. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Now the watchman standing on the tower in Jezreel saw \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*’s troops approaching, and he called out, “I see a company of troops!” 
\b
\m “Choose a rider,” \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* commanded. “Send him out to meet them and ask, ‘Have you come in peace?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So a horseman rode off to meet \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* and said, “This is what \x-wd the king|id="Q334739"\x-wd* asks: ‘Have you come in peace?’” 
\b
\m “What do you know about peace?” \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* replied. “Fall in behind me.” 
\b
\m And the watchman reported, “The messenger reached them, but he is not coming back.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 So \x-wd the king|id="Q334739"\x-wd* sent out a second horseman, who went to them and said, “This is what \x-wd the king|id="Q334739"\x-wd* asks: ‘Have you come in peace?’” 
\b
\m “What do you know about peace?” \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* replied. “Fall in behind me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Again the watchman reported, “He reached them, but he is not coming back. And the charioteer is driving like \x-wd Jehu son of Nimshi|id="Q313225"\x-wd* \f + \fr 9:20 \ft Or grandson of Nimshi; see verse 14.\f*—he is driving like a madman!” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 “Harness!” \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* shouted, and they harnessed his chariot. 
\b
\m Then \x-wd Joram king of Israel|id="Q334739"\x-wd* and \x-wd Ahaziah king of Judah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* set out, each in his own chariot, and met \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* on the property of \x-wd Naboth the Jezreelite|id="Q44203"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 When \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* saw \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, he asked, “Have you come in peace, \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*?” 
\b
\m “How can there be peace,” he replied, “as long as the idolatry \f + \fr 9:22 \ft Hebrew adultery or prostitution, here a metaphor for idolatry\f* and witchcraft of your mother \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd* abound?” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* turned around \f + \fr 9:23 \ft Literally turned his hands\f* and fled, calling out to \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*, “Treachery, \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* drew his bow and shot \x-wd Joram|id="Q334739"\x-wd* between the shoulders. The arrow pierced his heart, and he slumped down in his chariot. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Bidkar|id="Q133520484"\x-wd* his officer, “Pick him up and throw him into the field of \x-wd Naboth the Jezreelite|id="Q44203"\x-wd*. For remember that when you and I were riding together behind his father \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, the LORD lifted up this burden against him: 
\v 26 ‘As surely as I saw the blood of \x-wd Naboth|id="Q44203"\x-wd* and the blood of his sons yesterday, declares the LORD, so will I repay you on this plot of ground, declares the LORD.’ Now then, according to the word of the LORD, pick him up and throw him on the plot of ground.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When \x-wd King Ahaziah of Judah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* saw this, he fled up the road toward Beth-haggan. 
\b
\m And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* pursued him, shouting, “Shoot him too!” 
\b
\m So they shot \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* in his chariot on the Ascent of Gur,\f + \fr 9:27 \ft See Syriac, Vulgate, and LXX; Hebrew “Shoot him, too, in his chariot!” (They did this) on the Ascent of Gur,\f* near Ibleam, and he fled to Megiddo and died there. 
\v 28 Then his servants carried him by chariot to Jerusalem and buried him with his fathers in his tomb in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 (In the eleventh year of \x-wd Joram son of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*|id="Q334739"\x-wd*, \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* had become king over Judah.) 
\s1 Jezebel’s Violent Death
\b
\m 
\v 30 Now when \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* arrived in Jezreel, \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd* heard of it. So she painted her eyes, adorned her head, and looked down from a window. 
\v 31 And as \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* entered the gate, she asked, “Have you come in peace, O \x-wd Zimri|id="Q313230"\x-wd*, murderer of \x-wd your master|id="Q334739"\x-wd*?”\f + \fr 9:31 \ft Or “Is there peace for Zimri, the murderer of his master?” See 1 Kings 16:10.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 32 He looked up at the window and called out, “Who is on my side? Who?” 
\b
\m And two or three eunuchs looked down at him. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 “Throw her down!” yelled \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m So they threw her down, and her blood splattered on the wall and on the horses as they trampled her underfoot. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* went in and ate and drank. “Take care of \x-wd this cursed woman|id="Q721295"\x-wd*,” he said, “and bury her, for she was the daughter of a king.” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 But when they went out to bury her, they found nothing but her skull, her feet, and the palms of her hands. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 So they went back and told \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, who replied, “This is the word of the LORD, which He spoke through His servant \x-wd Elijah the Tishbite|id="Q133507"\x-wd*: ‘On the plot of ground at Jezreel the dogs will devour the flesh of \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd*. 
\v 37 And \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd*’s body will lie like dung in the field on the plot of ground at Jezreel, so that no one can say: This is \x-wd Jezebel|id="Q721295"\x-wd*.’\f + \fr 9:37 \ft See verse 10 and 1 Kings 21:23.\f*” 
\c 10
\s1 Ahab’s Seventy Sons Killed
\m 
\v 1 Now \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* had seventy sons in Samaria. So \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* wrote letters and sent them to Samaria to the officials of Jezreel,\f + \fr 10:1 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate officials of the city \f* to the elders, and to the guardians of the sons \f + \fr 10:1 \ft Hebrew does not include of the sons.\f* of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, saying: 
\v 2 “When this letter arrives, since \x-wd your master|id="Q235901"\x-wd*’s sons are with you and you have chariots and horses, a fortified city and weaponry, 
\v 3 select the best and most worthy son of \x-wd your master|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, set him on \x-wd his father|id="Q235901"\x-wd*’s throne, and fight for \x-wd your master|id="Q235901"\x-wd*’s house.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 But they were terrified and reasoned, “If \x-wd two kings|id="Q334739,Q313214"\x-wd* could not stand against him, how can we?” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So the palace administrator, the overseer of the city, the elders, and the guardians sent a message to \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*: “We are your servants, and we will do whatever you say. We will not make anyone king. Do whatever is good in your sight.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* wrote them a second letter and said: “If you are on my side, and if you will obey me, then bring the heads of \x-wd your master|id="Q235901"\x-wd*’s sons to me at Jezreel by this time tomorrow.” 
\b
\m Now the sons of \x-wd the king|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, seventy in all, were being brought up by the leading men of the city. 
\v 7 And when the letter arrived, they took the sons of \x-wd the king|id="Q235901"\x-wd* and slaughtered all seventy of them. They put their heads in baskets and sent them to \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* at Jezreel. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 When the messenger arrived, he told \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, “They have brought the heads of the sons of \x-wd the king|id="Q235901"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* ordered, “Pile them in two heaps at the entrance of the gate until morning.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 The next morning, \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* went out and stood before all the people and said, “You are innocent. It was I who conspired against \x-wd my master|id="Q334739"\x-wd* and killed him. But who killed all these? 
\v 10 Know, then, that not a word the LORD has spoken against the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* will fail, for the LORD has done what He promised through His servant \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* killed everyone in Jezreel who remained of the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, as well as all his great men and close friends and priests, leaving him without a single survivor. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* set out toward Samaria. At Beth-eked of the Shepherds, 
\v 13 \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* met some relatives of \x-wd Ahaziah king of Judah|id="Q313214"\x-wd* and asked, “Who are you?” 
\b
\m “We are relatives of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*,” they answered, “and we have come down to greet the sons of \x-wd the king|id="Q235901"\x-wd* and of the \x-wd queen mother|id="Q721295"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* ordered, “Take them alive.” So his men took them alive, then slaughtered them at the well of Beth-eked—forty-two men. He spared none of them. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When he left there, he found \x-wd Jehonadab son of \x-wd Rechab|id="Q124149671"\x-wd*|id="Q6176487"\x-wd*, who was coming to meet him. \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* greeted him and asked, “Is your heart as true to mine as my heart is to yours?” 
\b
\m “It is!” \x-wd Jehonadab|id="Q6176487"\x-wd* replied. 
\b
\m “If it is,” said \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, “give me your hand.” 
\b
\m So he gave him his hand, and \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* helped him into his chariot, 
\v 16 saying, “Come with me and see my zeal for the LORD!” So he had him ride \f + \fr 10:16 \ft LXX, Syriac, and Targum Yonaton; Hebrew they had him ride\f* in his chariot. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* came to Samaria, he struck down everyone belonging to \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* who remained there, until he had destroyed them, according to the word that the LORD had spoken to \x-wd Elijah|id="Q133507"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Jehu Kills the Priests of Baal
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* brought all the people together and said, “\x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* served Baal a little, but \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* will serve him a lot. 
\v 19 Now, therefore, summon to me all the prophets of Baal, all his servants, and all his priests. See that no one is missing, for I have a great sacrifice for Baal. Whoever is missing will not live.” 
\b
\m But \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* was acting deceptively in order to destroy the servants of Baal. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* commanded, “Proclaim a solemn assembly for Baal.” So they announced it. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* sent word throughout Israel, and all the servants of Baal came; there was not a man who failed to show. They entered the temple of Baal, and it was filled from end to end. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* said to the keeper of the wardrobe, “Bring out garments for all the servants of Baal.” So he brought out garments for them. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Next, \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* and \x-wd Jehonadab son of \x-wd Rechab|id="Q124149671"\x-wd*|id="Q6176487"\x-wd* entered the temple of Baal, and \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* said to the servants of Baal, “Look around to see that there are no servants of the LORD here among you—only servants of Baal.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 And they went in \f + \fr 10:24 \ft LXX he went in; see also verse 25.\f* to offer sacrifices and burnt offerings. Now \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* had stationed eighty men outside and warned them, “If anyone allows one of the men I am delivering into your hands to escape, he will forfeit his life for theirs.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 When he had finished making the burnt offering, \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* said to the guards and officers, “Go in and kill them. Do not let anyone out.” 
\b
\m So the guards and officers put them to the sword, threw the bodies out, and went into the inner room of the temple of Baal. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 They brought out the sacred pillar of the temple of Baal and burned it. 
\v 27 They also demolished the sacred pillar of Baal. Then they tore down the temple of Baal and made it into a latrine, which it is to this day. 
\s1 Jehu Repeats Jeroboam’s Sins
\b
\m 
\v 28 Thus \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* eradicated Baal from Israel, 
\v 29 but he did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit—the worship of the golden calves at Bethel and Dan. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Nevertheless, the LORD said to \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, “Because you have done well in carrying out what is right in My sight and have done to the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd* all that was in My heart, four generations of your sons will sit on the throne of Israel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Yet \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* was not careful to follow the instruction of the LORD, the God of Israel, with all his heart. He did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 In those days the LORD began to reduce the size of Israel. \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* defeated the Israelites throughout their territory 
\v 33 from the Jordan eastward through all the land of Gilead (the region of Gad, Reuben, and Manasseh), and from Aroer by the Arnon Valley through Gilead to Bashan. 
\s1 Jehoahaz Succeeds \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 34 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments and all his might, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 35 And \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried in Samaria, and his son \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\v 36 So the duration of \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*’s reign over Israel in Samaria was twenty-eight years. 
\c 11
\s1 Athaliah and Joash
\r (2 Chronicles 22:10–12)
\m 
\v 1 When \x-wd Athaliah the mother of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*|id="Q463934"\x-wd* saw that \x-wd her son|id="Q313214"\x-wd* was dead, she proceeded to annihilate all the royal heirs. 
\v 2 But \x-wd Jehosheba|id="Q4202829"\x-wd* \f + \fr 11:2 \ft Jehosheba is a variant of Jehoshabeath; see 2 Chronicles 22:11.\f* daughter of \x-wd King Joram|id="Q313215"\x-wd*,\f + \fr 11:2 \ft Joram is a variant spelling of Jehoram.\f* the sister of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*, took \x-wd Joash son of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*|id="Q313229"\x-wd* and stole him away from among the sons of the king who were being murdered. She put him and his nurse in a bedroom to hide him from \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd*, and he was not killed. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* remained hidden with his nurse in the house of the LORD for six years while \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd* ruled the land. 
\s1 Joash Anointed King of Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 23:1–11)
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then in the seventh year, \x-wd Jehoiada|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* sent for the commanders of hundreds, the Carites,\f + \fr 11:4 \ft Or the mercenaries or the executioners; also in verse 19\f* and the guards, and had them brought into the house of the LORD. There he made a covenant with them and put them under oath. 
\b
\m He showed them \x-wd \x-wd the king|id="Q334739"\x-wd*’s son|id="Q313229"\x-wd* 
\v 5 and commanded them, “This is what you are to do: A third of you who come on duty on the Sabbath shall guard the royal palace, 
\v 6 a third shall be at the gate of Sur, and a third at the gate behind the guards. You are to take turns guarding the temple—
\v 7 the two divisions that would go off duty on the Sabbath are to guard the house of the LORD for \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd*. 
\v 8 You must surround \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* with weapons in hand, and anyone who approaches the ranks must be put to death. You must stay close to \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* wherever he goes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So the commanders of hundreds did everything that \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* had ordered. Each of them took his men—those coming on duty on the Sabbath and those going off duty—and came to \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd*. 
\v 10 Then \x-wd the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* gave to the commanders of hundreds the spears and shields of \x-wd King David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* from the house of the LORD. 
\v 11 And the guards stood with weapons in hand surrounding \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* by the altar and the temple, from the south side to the north side of the temple. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \x-wd Jehoiada|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* brought out \x-wd \x-wd the king|id="Q334739"\x-wd*’s son|id="Q313229"\x-wd*, put the crown on him, presented him with the Testimony, and proclaimed him king. They anointed him, and the people clapped their hands and declared, “Long live \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd*!” 
\s1 The Death of Athaliah
\r (2 Chronicles 23:12–15)
\b
\m 
\v 13 When \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd* heard the noise from the guards and the people, she went out to the people in the house of the LORD. 
\v 14 And she looked out and saw \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* standing by the pillar, according to the custom. The officers and trumpeters were beside \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd*, and all the people of the land were rejoicing and blowing trumpets. 
\b
\m Then \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd* tore her clothes and screamed, “Treason! Treason!” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* ordered the commanders of hundreds in charge of the army, “Bring her out between the ranks,\f + \fr 11:15 \ft Or out from the precincts\f* and put to the sword anyone who follows her.” For the priest had said, “She must not be put to death in the house of the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So they seized \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd* as she reached the horses’ entrance to the palace grounds, and there she was put to death. 
\s1 Jehoiada Restores the Worship of the LORD
\r (2 Chronicles 23:16–21)
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \x-wd Jehoiada|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* made a covenant between the LORD and \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* and the people that they would be the LORD’s people. He also made a covenant between \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* and the people. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So all the people of the land went to the temple of Baal and tore it down. They smashed the altars and idols to pieces, and they killed \x-wd Mattan the priest of Baal|id="Q124129541"\x-wd* in front of the altars. 
\b
\m And \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* posted guards for the house of the LORD. 
\v 19 He took with him the commanders of hundreds, the Carites, the guards, and all the people of the land, and they brought \x-wd the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd* down from the house of the LORD and entered the royal palace by way of the Gate of the Guards. 
\b
\m Then \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* took his seat on the royal throne, 
\v 20 and all the people of the land rejoiced. And the city was quiet, because \x-wd Athaliah|id="Q463934"\x-wd* had been put to the sword at the royal palace. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* \f + \fr 11:21 \ft Hebrew Jehoash, a variant of Joash (son of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*) as in verse 2\f* was seven years old when he became king. 
\c 12
\s1 Joash Repairs the Temple
\r (2 Chronicles 24:1–14)
\m 
\v 1 In the seventh year of \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*, \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* \f + \fr 12:1 \ft Hebrew Jehoash, a variant of Joash (son of Ahaziah); also in verses 2, 4, 6, 7, and 18; see 2 Kings 11:2.\f* became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem forty years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Zibiah|id="Q8071446"\x-wd*; she was from Beersheba. 
\v 2 And \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* did what was right in the eyes of the LORD all the days he was instructed by \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Nevertheless, the high places were not removed; the people continued sacrificing and burning incense there. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* said to the priests, “Collect all the money brought as sacred gifts into the house of the LORD—the census money, the money from vows, and the money brought voluntarily into the house of the LORD. 
\v 5 Let every priest receive it from his constituency, and let it be used to repair any damage found in the temple.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 By the twenty-third year of the reign of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*, however, the priests had not yet repaired the damage to the temple. 
\v 7 So \x-wd King Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* called \x-wd Jehoiada|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* and the other priests and said, “Why have you not repaired the damage to the temple? Now, therefore, take no more money from your constituency, but hand it over for the repair of the temple.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So the priests agreed that they would not receive money from the people and that they would not repair the temple themselves. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \x-wd Jehoiada the priest|id="Q2194240"\x-wd* took a chest, bored a hole in its lid, and set it beside the altar on the right side as one enters the house of the LORD. There the priests who guarded the threshold put all the money brought into the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Whenever they saw that there was a large amount of money in the chest, the royal scribe and the high priest would go up, count the money brought into the house of the LORD, and tie it up in bags. 
\v 11 Then they would put the counted money into the hands of those who supervised the work on the house of the LORD, who in turn would pay those doing the work—the carpenters, builders, 
\v 12 masons, and stonecutters. They also purchased timber and dressed stone to repair the damage to the house of the LORD, and they paid the other expenses of the temple repairs. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 However, the money brought into the house of the LORD was not used for making silver basins, wick trimmers, sprinkling bowls, trumpets, or any articles of gold or silver for the house of the LORD. 
\v 14 Instead, it was paid to those doing the work, and with it they repaired the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 No accounting was required from the men who received the money to pay the workmen, because they acted with integrity. 
\v 16 The money from the guilt offerings and sin offerings was not brought into the house of the LORD; it belonged to the priests. 
\s1 The Death of Joash
\r (2 Chronicles 24:23–27)
\b
\m 
\v 17 At that time \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* marched up and fought against Gath and captured it. Then he decided to attack Jerusalem. 
\v 18 So \x-wd King Joash of Judah|id="Q313229"\x-wd* took all the sacred objects dedicated by his fathers—\x-wd Jehoshaphat|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, \x-wd Jehoram|id="Q313215"\x-wd*, and \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*, the kings of Judah—along with his own consecrated items and all the gold found in the treasuries of the house of the LORD and the royal palace, and he sent them to \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd*. So \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* withdrew from Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And the servants of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* rose up and formed a conspiracy and killed him at Beth-millo, on the road down to Silla. 
\v 21 His servants \x-wd Jozabad \f + \fr 12:21 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Syriac Jozacar\f* son of \x-wd Shimeath|id="Q133815999"\x-wd*|id="Q133760827"\x-wd* and \x-wd Jehozabad son of \x-wd Shomer|id="Q137824425"\x-wd*|id="Q133760684"\x-wd* \f + \fr 12:21 \ft Shomer is a variant of Shimrith; see 2 Chronicles 24:26.\f* struck him down, and he died. And they buried him with his fathers in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*, and his son \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\c 13
\s1 Jehoahaz Reigns in Israel
\m 
\v 1 In the twenty-third year of the reign of \x-wd Joash son of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*|id="Q313229"\x-wd* over Judah, \x-wd Jehoahaz son of \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*|id="Q334729"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria seventeen years. 
\v 2 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and followed the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit; he did not turn away from them. 
\v 3 So the anger of the LORD burned against Israel, and He delivered them continually into the hands of \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* and his son \x-wd Ben-hadad|id="Q816398"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd* sought the favor of the LORD, and the LORD listened to him because He saw the oppression that \x-wd the king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* had inflicted on Israel. 
\v 5 So the LORD gave Israel a deliverer, and they escaped the power of the Arameans. Then the people of Israel lived in their own homes as they had before. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Nevertheless, they did not turn away from the sins that the house of \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit, but they continued to walk in them.\f + \fr 13:6 \ft LXX, Syriac, Targum Yonaton, and Vulgate; Hebrew he continued to walk in them\f* The Asherah pole even remained standing in Samaria. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd* had no army left, except fifty horsemen, ten chariots, and ten thousand foot soldiers, because \x-wd the king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* had destroyed them and made them like the dust at threshing. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments and his might, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried in Samaria. And his son \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* \f + \fr 13:9 \ft Hebrew Joash, a variant of Jehoash; also in verses 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, and 25\f* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Jehoash Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 10 In the thirty-seventh year of the reign of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* over Judah, \x-wd Jehoash son of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*|id="Q334736"\x-wd* became king of Israel in Samaria, and he reigned sixteen years. 
\v 11 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and did not turn away from all the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit, but he walked in them. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments and his might, including his war against \x-wd Amaziah king of Judah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* rested with his fathers, and \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd* succeeded him on the throne. \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* was buried in Samaria with the kings of Israel. 
\s1 Elisha’s Final Prophecy
\b
\m 
\v 14 When \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* had fallen sick with the illness from which he would die, \x-wd Jehoash king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd* came down to him and wept over him, saying, “My father, my father, the chariots and horsemen of Israel!” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* told him, “Take a bow and some arrows.” 
\b
\m So \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* took a bow and some arrows. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said to \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd*, “Put your hand on the bow.” 
\b
\m So \x-wd the king|id="Q334736"\x-wd* put his hand on the bow, and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* put his hands on \x-wd the king|id="Q334736"\x-wd*’s hands. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “Open the east window,” said \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m So he opened it and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Shoot!” So he shot. 
\b
\m And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* declared: 
\b
\q1 “This is the LORD’s arrow of victory, 
\q2 the arrow of victory over Aram, 
\q1 for you shall strike the Arameans in Aphek 
\q2 until you have put an end to them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said, “Take the arrows!” 
\b
\m So he took them, and \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* said to \x-wd the king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd*, “Strike the ground!” 
\b
\m So he struck the ground three times and stopped. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But \x-wd the man of God|id="Q206238"\x-wd* was angry with him and said, “You should have struck the ground five or six times. Then you would have struck down Aram until you had put an end to it. But now you will strike down Aram only three times.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd* died and was buried. 
\b
\m Now the Moabite raiders used to come into the land every spring.\f + \fr 13:20 \ft Literally into the land at the coming in of the year\f* 
\v 21 Once, as the Israelites were burying a man, suddenly they saw a band of raiders, so they threw the man’s body into \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*’s tomb. And as soon as his body touched the bones of \x-wd Elisha|id="Q206238"\x-wd*, the man was revived and stood up on his feet. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* oppressed Israel throughout the reign of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*. 
\v 23 But the LORD was gracious to Israel and had compassion on them, and He turned toward them because of His covenant with \x-wd Abraham|id="Q9181"\x-wd*, \x-wd Isaac|id="Q671872"\x-wd*, and \x-wd Jacob|id="Q289957"\x-wd*. And to this day, the LORD has been unwilling to destroy them or cast them from His presence. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 When \x-wd Hazael king of Aram|id="Q127170"\x-wd* died, his son \x-wd Ben-hadad|id="Q816398"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\v 25 Then \x-wd Jehoash son of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*|id="Q334736"\x-wd* took back from \x-wd Ben-hadad son of \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd*|id="Q816398"\x-wd* the cities that \x-wd Hazael|id="Q127170"\x-wd* had taken in battle from \x-wd his father Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*. \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* defeated \x-wd Ben-hadad|id="Q816398"\x-wd* three times, and so recovered the cities of Israel. 
\c 14
\s1 Amaziah Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 25:1–4)
\m 
\v 1 In the second year of the reign of \x-wd Jehoash \f + \fr 14:1 \ft Hebrew Joash, a variant of Jehoash; also in verses 13, 23, and 27\f* son of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*|id="Q334736"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Amaziah son of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*|id="Q313237"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 2 He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem twenty-nine years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Jehoaddan|id="Q20502568"\x-wd*; she was from Jerusalem. 
\v 3 And he did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, but not as his father \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* had done. He did everything as \x-wd his father Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd* had done. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Nevertheless, the high places were not taken away, and the people continued sacrificing and burning incense on the high places. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 As soon as the kingdom was firmly in his grasp, \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* executed \x-wd the servants|id="Q313229,Q133760827"\x-wd* who had murdered \x-wd his father the king|id="Q313229"\x-wd*. 
\v 6 Yet he did not put the sons of \x-wd the murderers|id="Q313229,Q133760827"\x-wd* to death, but acted according to what is written in the Book of the Law of \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd*, where the LORD commanded: “Fathers must not be put to death for their children, and children must not be put to death for their fathers; each is to die for his own sin.”\f + \fr 14:6 \ft Deuteronomy 24:16\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* struck down 10,000 Edomites in the Valley of Salt. He took Sela in battle and called it Joktheel, which is its name to this very day. 
\s1 Jehoash Defeats Amaziah
\r (2 Chronicles 25:17–24)
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* sent messengers to the king of Israel \x-wd Jehoash son of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*|id="Q334736"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd*. “Come, let us meet face to face,” he said. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \x-wd Jehoash king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd* replied to \x-wd Amaziah king of Judah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*: “A thistle in Lebanon sent a message to a cedar in Lebanon, saying, ‘Give your daughter to my son in marriage.’ Then a wild beast in Lebanon came along and trampled the thistle. 
\v 10 You have indeed defeated Edom, and your heart has become proud. Glory in that and stay at home. Why should you stir up trouble so that you fall—you and Judah with you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 But \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* would not listen, so \x-wd Jehoash king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd* advanced. He and \x-wd King Amaziah of Judah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* faced each other at Beth-shemesh in Judah. 
\v 12 And Judah was routed before Israel, and every man fled to his home. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 There at Beth-shemesh, \x-wd Jehoash king of Israel|id="Q334736"\x-wd* captured \x-wd Amaziah king of Judah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Ahaziah|id="Q313214"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m Then \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* went to Jerusalem and broke down the wall of Jerusalem from the Ephraim Gate to the Corner Gate—a section of four hundred cubits.\f + \fr 14:13 \ft 400 cubits is approximately 600 feet or 182.9 meters.\f* 
\v 14 He took all the gold and silver and all the articles found in the house of the LORD and in the treasuries of the royal palace, as well as some hostages. Then he returned to Samaria. 
\s1 Jeroboam II Succeeds Jehoash in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 15 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd*, along with his accomplishments, his might, and how he waged war against \x-wd Amaziah king of Judah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried in Samaria with the kings of Israel. And his son \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 The Death of Amaziah
\r (2 Chronicles 25:25–28)
\b
\m 
\v 17 \x-wd Amaziah son of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*|id="Q313237"\x-wd* king of Judah lived for fifteen years after the death of \x-wd Jehoash son of \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q334729"\x-wd*|id="Q334736"\x-wd* king of Israel. 
\v 18 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And conspirators plotted against \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* in Jerusalem, and he fled to Lachish. But men were sent after him to Lachish, and they killed him there. 
\v 20 They carried him back on horses and buried him in Jerusalem with his fathers in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Azariah Succeeds Amaziah in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 26:1–2)
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then all the people of Judah took \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*,\f + \fr 14:21 \ft Azariah is also called Uzziah; see 2 Chronicles 26:1.\f* who was sixteen years old, and made him king in place of \x-wd his father Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*. 
\v 22 \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd* was the one who rebuilt Elath \f + \fr 14:22 \ft Elath is a variant of Eloth; see 1 Kings 9:26.\f* and restored it to Judah after \x-wd King Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* rested with his fathers. 
\s1 Jeroboam II Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 23 In the fifteenth year of the reign of \x-wd Amaziah son of \x-wd Joash|id="Q313229"\x-wd*|id="Q313237"\x-wd* over Judah, \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd*|id="Q313232"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria forty-one years. 
\v 24 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and did not turn away from all the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 This \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd* restored the boundary of Israel from Lebo-hamath to the Sea of the Arabah,\f + \fr 14:25 \ft That is, the Dead Sea\f* according to the word that the LORD, the God of Israel, had spoken through His servant Jonah son of Amittai, the prophet from Gath-hepher. 
\v 26 For the LORD saw that the affliction of the Israelites, both slave and free, was very bitter. There was no one to help Israel, 
\v 27 and since the LORD had said that He would not blot out the name of Israel from under heaven, He saved them by the hand of \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Jehoash|id="Q334736"\x-wd*|id="Q313232"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments and might, and how he waged war and recovered both Damascus and Hamath for Israel from Judah, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 29 And \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd* rested with his fathers,\f + \fr 14:29 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts include and he was buried in Samaria with\f* the kings of Israel. And his son Zechariah reigned in his place. 
\c 15
\s1 Azariah Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 26:3–23)
\m 
\v 1 In the twenty-seventh year of \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd*’s reign over Israel, \x-wd Azariah \f + \fr 15:1 \ft Azariah is also called Uzziah; also in verses 6, 7, 8, 17, 23, and 27; see 2 Chronicles 26:1.\f* son of \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd*|id="Q313216"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 2 He was sixteen years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem fifty-two years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Jecoliah|id="Q20502646"\x-wd*; she was from Jerusalem. 
\v 3 And he did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, just as his father \x-wd Amaziah|id="Q313237"\x-wd* had done. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Nevertheless, the high places were not taken away; the people continued sacrificing and burning incense there. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And the LORD afflicted \x-wd the king|id="Q313216"\x-wd* with leprosy \f + \fr 15:5 \ft Leprosy was a term used for various skin diseases; see Leviticus 13.\f* until the day he died, so that he lived in a separate house while his son \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd* had charge of the palace and governed the people of the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried near them\f + \fr 15:7 \ft Literally with his fathers; see 2 Chronicles 26:23.\f* in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*. And his son \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Zechariah Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 8 In the thirty-eighth year of \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*’s reign over Judah, \x-wd Zechariah son of \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313232"\x-wd*|id="Q334726"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria six months. 
\v 9 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, as his fathers had done. He did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \x-wd Shallum son of \x-wd Jabesh|id="Q16947750"\x-wd*|id="Q334748"\x-wd* conspired against \x-wd Zechariah|id="Q334726"\x-wd*, struck him down and killed him in front of the people,\f + \fr 15:10 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts in Ibleam\f* and reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Zechariah|id="Q334726"\x-wd*, they are indeed written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel. 
\v 12 So the word of the LORD spoken to \x-wd Jehu|id="Q313225"\x-wd* was fulfilled: “Four generations of your sons will sit on the throne of Israel.” 
\s1 Shallum Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 13 In the thirty-ninth year of \x-wd Uzziah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*’s \f + \fr 15:13 \ft Uzziah is also called Azariah; also in verses 32 and 34; see verse 1.\f* reign over Judah, \x-wd Shallum son of \x-wd Jabesh|id="Q16947750"\x-wd*|id="Q334748"\x-wd* became king, and he reigned in Samaria one full month. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \x-wd Menahem son of \x-wd Gadi|id="Q114906601"\x-wd*|id="Q313212"\x-wd* went up from Tirzah to Samaria, struck down and killed \x-wd Shallum son of \x-wd Jabesh|id="Q16947750"\x-wd*|id="Q334748"\x-wd*, and reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Shallum|id="Q334748"\x-wd*, along with the conspiracy he led, they are indeed written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 At that time \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd*, starting from Tirzah, attacked Tiphsah and everyone in its vicinity, because they would not open their gates. So he attacked Tiphsah and ripped open all the pregnant women. 
\s1 Menahem Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 17 In the thirty-ninth year of \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*’s reign over Judah, \x-wd Menahem son of \x-wd Gadi|id="Q114906601"\x-wd*|id="Q313212"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria ten years. 
\v 18 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, and throughout his reign he did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then \x-wd Pul \f + \fr 15:19 \ft Pul is another name for Tiglath-pileser; see verse 29.\f* king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd* invaded the land, and \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd* gave \x-wd Pul|id="Q313212"\x-wd* a thousand talents of silver \f + \fr 15:19 \ft 1,000 talents is approximately 37.7 tons or 34.2 metric tons of silver.\f* in order to gain his support and strengthen his own grip on the kingdom. 
\v 20 \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd* exacted this money from each of the wealthy men of Israel—fifty shekels of silver \f + \fr 15:20 \ft 50 shekels is approximately 1.26 pounds or 569.8 grams of silver.\f* from each man—to give to the king of Assyria. So the king of Assyria withdrew and did not remain in the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel? 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd* rested with his fathers, and his son \x-wd Pekahiah|id="Q334756"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Pekahiah Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 23 In the fiftieth year of \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*’s reign over Judah, \x-wd Pekahiah son of \x-wd Menahem|id="Q313212"\x-wd*|id="Q334756"\x-wd* became king of Israel and reigned in Samaria two years. 
\v 24 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then his officer, \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd*, conspired against him along with \x-wd Argob|id="Q131839413"\x-wd*, \x-wd Arieh|id="Q131839428"\x-wd*, and fifty men of Gilead. And at the citadel of the king’s palace in Samaria, \x-wd Pekah|id="Q334744"\x-wd* struck down and killed \x-wd Pekahiah|id="Q334756"\x-wd* and reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Pekahiah|id="Q334756"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, they are indeed written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel. 
\s1 Pekah Reigns in Israel
\b
\m 
\v 27 In the fifty-second year of \x-wd Azariah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*’s reign over Judah, \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria twenty years. 
\v 28 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD and did not turn away from the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had caused Israel to commit. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 In the days of \x-wd Pekah king of Israel|id="Q334744"\x-wd*, \x-wd Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria|id="Q210074"\x-wd* came and captured Ijon, Abel-beth-maacah, Janoah, Kedesh, Hazor, Gilead, and Galilee, including all the land of Naphtali, and he took the people as captives to Assyria. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \x-wd Hoshea son of \x-wd Elah|id="Q124309504"\x-wd*|id="Q319038"\x-wd* led a conspiracy against \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd*. In the twentieth year of \x-wd Jotham son of \x-wd Uzziah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*|id="Q109920"\x-wd*, \x-wd Hoshea|id="Q319038"\x-wd* attacked \x-wd Pekah|id="Q334744"\x-wd*, killed him, and reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Pekah|id="Q334744"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, they are indeed written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Israel. 
\s1 Jotham Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 27:1–9)
\b
\m 
\v 32 In the second year of the reign of \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Jotham son of \x-wd Uzziah|id="Q313216"\x-wd*|id="Q109920"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 33 He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem sixteen years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Jerusha \f + \fr 15:33 \ft Jerusha is a variant of Jerushah; see 2 Chronicles 27:1.\f* daughter of \x-wd Zadok|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q137824550"\x-wd*. 
\v 34 And he did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, just as his father \x-wd Uzziah|id="Q313216"\x-wd* had done. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 Nevertheless, the high places were not taken away; the people continued sacrificing and burning incense there. 
\b
\m \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd* rebuilt the Upper Gate of the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd*, along with his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 37 (In those days the LORD began to send \x-wd Rezin king of Aram|id="Q878947"\x-wd* and \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd* against Judah.) 
\b
\m 
\v 38 And \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* his father. And his son Ahaz reigned in his place. 
\c 16
\s1 Ahaz Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 28:1–4)
\m 
\v 1 In the seventeenth year of \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd*, \x-wd Ahaz son of \x-wd Jotham|id="Q109920"\x-wd*|id="Q30914"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 2 \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* was twenty years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem sixteen years. And unlike \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* his father, he did not do what was right in the eyes of the LORD his God. 
\v 3 Instead, he walked in the ways of the kings of Israel and even sacrificed his son in the fire,\f + \fr 16:3 \ft Literally made his son pass through the fire\f* according to the abominations of the nations that the LORD had driven out before the Israelites. 
\v 4 And he sacrificed and burned incense on the high places, on the hills, and under every green tree. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \x-wd Rezin king of Aram|id="Q878947"\x-wd* and \x-wd Pekah son of \x-wd Remaliah|id="Q114906785"\x-wd*|id="Q334744"\x-wd* king of Israel came up to wage war against Jerusalem. They besieged \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* but could not overcome him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 At that time \x-wd Rezin king of Aram|id="Q878947"\x-wd* recovered Elath \f + \fr 16:6 \ft Elath is a variant of Eloth; see 1 Kings 9:26.\f* for Aram,\f + \fr 16:6 \ft Or Syria\f* drove out the men of Judah, and sent the Edomites into Elath, where they live to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 So \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* sent messengers to \x-wd Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria|id="Q210074"\x-wd*, saying, “I am your servant and your son. Come up and save me from the hands of the kings of Aram and Israel, who are rising up against me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* also took the silver and gold found in the house of the LORD and in the treasuries of the king’s palace, and he sent it as a gift to the king of Assyria. 
\v 9 So \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q210074"\x-wd* responded to him, marched up to Damascus, and captured it. He took its people to Kir as captives and put \x-wd Rezin|id="Q878947"\x-wd* to death. 
\s1 The Idolatry of Ahaz
\r (2 Chronicles 28:16–27)
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* went to Damascus to meet \x-wd Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria|id="Q210074"\x-wd*. On seeing the altar in Damascus, \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* sent \x-wd Uriah the priest|id="Q104898031"\x-wd* a model of the altar and complete plans for its construction. 
\v 11 And \x-wd Uriah the priest|id="Q104898031"\x-wd* built the altar according to all the instructions \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* had sent from Damascus, and he completed it before \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* returned. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 When \x-wd the king|id="Q30914"\x-wd* came back from Damascus and saw the altar, he approached it and presented offerings on it. 
\v 13 He offered his burnt offering and his grain offering, poured out his drink offering, and splattered the blood of his peace offerings on the altar. 
\v 14 He also took the bronze altar that stood before the LORD from the front of the temple (between the new altar and the house of the LORD) and he put it on the north side of the new altar. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* commanded \x-wd Uriah the priest|id="Q104898031"\x-wd*, “Offer on the great altar the morning burnt offering, the evening grain offering, and the king’s burnt offering and grain offering, as well as the burnt offerings, grain offerings, and drink offerings of all the people of the land. Splatter on the altar all the blood of the burnt offerings and sacrifices. But I will use the bronze altar to seek guidance.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \x-wd Uriah the priest|id="Q104898031"\x-wd* did just as \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* had commanded. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 \x-wd King Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* also cut off the frames of the movable stands and removed the bronze basin from each of them. He took down the Sea from the bronze oxen that were under it and put it on a stone base. 
\v 18 And on account of the \x-wd king of Assyria|id="Q210074"\x-wd*, he removed the Sabbath canopy \f + \fr 16:18 \ft Or the base of his throne; see also LXX.\f* they had built in the temple and closed the royal entryway outside the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd*, along with his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the City of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*, and his son \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\c 17
\s1 Hoshea the Last King of Israel
\m 
\v 1 In the twelfth year of the reign of \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd* over Judah, \x-wd Hoshea son of \x-wd Elah|id="Q124309504"\x-wd*|id="Q319038"\x-wd* became king of Israel, and he reigned in Samaria nine years. 
\v 2 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, but not like the kings of Israel who preceded him. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \x-wd Shalmaneser king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* attacked him, and \x-wd Hoshea|id="Q319038"\x-wd* became his vassal and paid him tribute. 
\v 4 But \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* discovered that \x-wd Hoshea|id="Q319038"\x-wd* had conspired to send envoys to King So \f + \fr 17:4 \ft So is likely an abbreviation for Osorkon.\f* of Egypt, and that he had not paid tribute to \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* as in previous years. Therefore \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* arrested \x-wd Hoshea|id="Q319038"\x-wd* and put him in prison. 
\s1 Israel Carried Captive to Assyria
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then the \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* invaded the whole land, marched up to Samaria, and besieged it for three years. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 In the ninth year of \x-wd Hoshea|id="Q319038"\x-wd*, the \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* captured Samaria and carried away the Israelites to Assyria, where he settled them in Halah, in Gozan by the Habor River, and in the cities of the Medes. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 All this happened because the people of Israel had sinned against the LORD their God, who had brought them out of the land of Egypt from under the hand of \x-wd Pharaoh king of Egypt|id=""\x-wd*. They had worshiped other gods 
\v 8 and walked in the customs of the nations that the LORD had driven out before the Israelites, as well as in the practices introduced by the kings of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 The Israelites secretly did things against the LORD their God that were not right. From watchtower to fortified city, they built high places in all their cities. 
\v 10 They set up for themselves sacred pillars and Asherah poles on every high hill and under every green tree. 
\v 11 They burned incense on all the high places like the nations that the LORD had driven out before them. They did wicked things, provoking the LORD to anger. 
\v 12 They served idols, although the LORD had told them, “You shall not do this thing.”\f + \fr 17:12 \ft Exodus 20:4–6; Deuteronomy 5:8–10\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Yet through all His prophets and seers, the LORD warned Israel and Judah, saying, “Turn from your wicked ways and keep My commandments and statutes, according to the entire Law that I commanded your fathers and delivered to you through My servants the prophets.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But they would not listen, and they stiffened their necks like their fathers, who did not believe the LORD their God. 
\v 15 They rejected His statutes and the covenant He had made with their fathers, as well as the decrees He had given them. They pursued worthless idols and became worthless themselves, going after the surrounding nations that the LORD had commanded them not to imitate. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 They abandoned all the commandments of the LORD their God and made for themselves two cast idols of calves and an Asherah pole. They bowed down to all the host of heaven and served Baal. 
\v 17 They sacrificed their sons and daughters in the fire \f + \fr 17:17 \ft Literally made their sons and their daughters pass through the fire\f* and practiced divination and soothsaying. They devoted themselves to doing evil in the sight of the LORD, provoking Him to anger. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So the LORD was very angry with Israel, and He removed them from His presence. Only the tribe of Judah remained, 
\v 19 and even Judah did not keep the commandments of the LORD their God, but lived according to the customs Israel had introduced. 
\v 20 So the LORD rejected all the descendants of Israel. He afflicted them and delivered them into the hands of plunderers, until He had banished them from His presence. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When the LORD had torn Israel away from the house of \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*, they made \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd* king, and \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313219"\x-wd* led Israel away from following the LORD and caused them to commit a great sin. 
\v 22 The Israelites persisted in all the sins that \x-wd Jeroboam|id="Q313219"\x-wd* had committed and did not turn away from them. 
\v 23 Finally, the LORD removed Israel from His presence, as He had declared through all His servants the prophets. So Israel was exiled from their homeland into Assyria, where they are to this day. 
\s1 Samaria Resettled
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \x-wd the king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd* brought people from Babylon, Cuthah, Avva, Hamath, and Sepharvaim and settled them in the towns of Samaria to replace the Israelites. They took possession of Samaria and lived in its towns. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Now when the settlers first lived there, they did not worship the LORD, so He sent lions among them, which killed some of them. 
\v 26 So they spoke to the \x-wd the king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd*, saying, “The peoples that you have removed and placed in the cities of Samaria do not know the requirements of the God of the land. Because of this, He has sent lions among them, which are indeed killing them off.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then the \x-wd the king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd* commanded: “Send back one of the priests you carried off from Samaria, and have him go back to live there and teach the requirements of the God of the land.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Thus one of the priests they had carried away came and lived in Bethel, and he began to teach them how they should worship the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Nevertheless, the people of each nation continued to make their own gods in the cities where they had settled, and they set them up in the shrines that the people of Samaria had made on the high places. 
\v 30 The men of Babylon made Succoth-benoth, the men of Cuth made Nergal, the men of Hamath made Ashima, 
\v 31 the Avvites made Nibhaz and Tartak, and the Sepharvites burned their children in the fire to Adrammelech and Anammelech the gods of Sepharvaim. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 So the new residents worshiped the LORD, but they also appointed for themselves priests of all sorts to serve in the shrines of the high places. 
\v 33 They worshiped the LORD, but they also served their own gods according to the customs of the nations from which they had been carried away. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 To this day they are still practicing their former customs. None of them worship the LORD or observe the statutes, ordinances, laws, and commandments that the LORD gave the descendants of \x-wd Jacob|id="Q289957"\x-wd*, whom He named \x-wd Israel|id="Q289957"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 For the LORD had made a covenant with the Israelites and commanded them, “Do not worship other gods or bow down to them; do not serve them or sacrifice to them. 
\v 36 Instead, worship the LORD, who brought you out of the land of Egypt with great power and an outstretched arm. You are to bow down to Him and offer sacrifices to Him. 
\v 37 And you must always be careful to observe the statutes, ordinances, laws, and commandments He wrote for you. Do not worship other gods. 
\v 38 Do not forget the covenant I have made with you. Do not worship other gods, 
\v 39 but worship the LORD your God, and He will deliver you from the hands of all your enemies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 40 But they would not listen, and they persisted in their former customs. 
\v 41 So these nations worshiped the LORD but also served their idols, and to this day their children and grandchildren continue to do as their fathers did. 
\c 18
\s1 Hezekiah Destroys Idolatry in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 29:1–2)
\m 
\v 1 In the third year of the reign of \x-wd Hoshea son of \x-wd Elah|id="Q124309504"\x-wd*|id="Q319038"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Hezekiah son of \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd*|id="Q244912"\x-wd* became king of Judah. 
\v 2 He was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem twenty-nine years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Abi,\f + \fr 18:2 \ft Abi is a variant of Abijah; see 2 Chronicles 29:1.\f* the daughter of \x-wd Zechariah|id="Q123167260"\x-wd*|id="Q3603705"\x-wd*. 
\v 3 And he did what was right in the eyes of the LORD, just as his father \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* had done. 
\v 4 He removed the high places, shattered the sacred pillars, and cut down the Asherah poles. He also demolished the bronze snake called Nehushtan \f + \fr 18:4 \ft Nehushtan sounds like the Hebrew for bronze and also for snake.\f* that \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd* had made, for up to that time the Israelites had burned incense to it. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* trusted in the LORD, the God of Israel. No king of Judah was like him, either before him or after him. 
\v 6 He remained faithful to the LORD and did not turn from following Him; he kept the commandments that the LORD had given \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And the LORD was with \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, and he prospered wherever he went. He rebelled against the \x-wd the king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd* and refused to serve him. 
\v 8 He defeated the Philistines as far as Gaza and its borders, from watchtower to fortified city. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 In the fourth year of \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*’s reign, which was the seventh year of the reign of \x-wd Hoshea son of \x-wd Elah|id="Q124309504"\x-wd*|id="Q319038"\x-wd* over Israel, \x-wd Shalmaneser king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* marched against Samaria and besieged it. 
\v 10 And at the end of three years, the Assyrians captured it. 
\b
\m So Samaria was captured in the sixth year of \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, which was the ninth year of \x-wd Hoshea king of Israel|id="Q319038"\x-wd*. 
\v 11 \x-wd The king of Assyria|id="Q310640"\x-wd* exiled the Israelites to Assyria and settled them in Halah, in Gozan by the Habor River, and in the cities of the Medes. 
\v 12 This happened because they did not listen to the voice of the LORD their God, but violated His covenant—all that \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd* the servant of the LORD had commanded—and would neither listen nor obey. 
\s1 Sennacherib Invades Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 32:1–8; Psalms 46:1–11)
\b
\m 
\v 13 In the fourteenth year of \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*’s reign, \x-wd Sennacherib king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* attacked and captured all the fortified cities of Judah. 
\v 14 So \x-wd Hezekiah king of Judah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* sent word to \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* at Lachish, saying, “I have done wrong; withdraw from me, and I will pay whatever you demand from me.” 
\b
\m And \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* exacted from \x-wd Hezekiah king of Judah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* three hundred talents of silver \f + \fr 18:14 \ft 300 talents is approximately 11.3 tons or 10.3 metric tons of silver.\f* and thirty talents of gold.\f + \fr 18:14 \ft 30 talents is approximately 1.13 tons or 1.03 metric tons of gold.\f* 
\v 15 \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* gave him all the silver that was found in the house of the LORD and in the treasuries of the royal palace. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 At that time \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* stripped the gold with which he had plated the doors and doorposts of the temple of the LORD, and he gave it to \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Sennacherib Threatens Jerusalem
\r (2 Chronicles 32:9–19; Isaiah 36:1–22)
\b
\m 
\v 17 Nevertheless, \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* sent the Tartan,\f + \fr 18:17 \ft Hebrew Tartan is the title of a field marshal, general, or commander in the Assyrian military.\f* the Rabsaris,\f + \fr 18:17 \ft Hebrew Rabsaris is the title of the chief eunuch in the Assyrian military.\f* and the Rabshakeh,\f + \fr 18:17 \ft Hebrew Rabshakeh is the title of a high-ranking Assyrian military officer; here and throughout chapters 18 and 19, as well as Isaiah 36 and 37.\f* along with a great army, from Lachish to \x-wd King Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* at Jerusalem. They advanced up to Jerusalem and stationed themselves by the aqueduct of the upper pool, on the road to the Launderer’s Field. 
\v 18 Then they called for the king. And \x-wd Eliakim son of \x-wd Hilkiah|id="Q130270587"\x-wd*|id="Q244912"\x-wd* the palace administrator, \x-wd Shebnah the scribe|id=""\x-wd*, and \x-wd Joah son of \x-wd Asaph|id="Q130243561"\x-wd*|id="Q130271252"\x-wd* the recorder, went out to them. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 The Rabshakeh said to them, “Tell \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* that this is what \x-wd the great king|id="Q207140"\x-wd*, \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*, says: What is the basis of this confidence of yours? 
\v 20 You claim to have a strategy and strength for war, but these are empty words. In whom are you now trusting, that you have rebelled against me? 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Look now, you are trusting in Egypt, that splintered reed of a staff that will pierce the hand of anyone who leans on it. Such is \x-wd Pharaoh king of Egypt|id=""\x-wd* to all who trust in him. 
\v 22 But if you say to me, ‘We trust in the LORD our God,’ is He not the One whose high places and altars \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* has removed, saying to Judah and Jerusalem: ‘You must worship before this altar in Jerusalem’? 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Now, therefore, make a bargain with \x-wd my master|id="Q207140"\x-wd*, \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*. I will give you two thousand horses—if you can put riders on them! 
\v 24 For how can you repel a single officer among the least of \x-wd my master|id="Q207140"\x-wd*’s servants when you depend on Egypt for chariots and horsemen? 
\v 25 So now, was it apart from the LORD that I have come up against this place to destroy it? The LORD Himself said to me, ‘Go up against this land and destroy it.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then \x-wd Eliakim son of \x-wd Hilkiah|id="Q130270587"\x-wd*|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, along with \x-wd Shebnah|id=""\x-wd* and \x-wd Joah|id="Q130271252"\x-wd*, said to the Rabshakeh, “Please speak to your servants in Aramaic, since we understand it. Do not speak with us in Hebrew \f + \fr 18:26 \ft Or in the dialect of Judah; also in verse 28\f* in the hearing of the people on the wall.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 But the Rabshakeh replied, “Has \x-wd my master|id="Q207140"\x-wd* sent me to speak these words only to you and your master, and not to the men sitting on the wall, who are destined with you to eat their own dung and drink their own urine?” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then the Rabshakeh stood and called out loudly in Hebrew: “Hear the word of \x-wd the great king|id="Q207140"\x-wd*, \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*! 
\v 29 This is what \x-wd the king|id="Q207140"\x-wd* says: Do not let \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* deceive you; he cannot deliver you from my hand. 
\v 30 Do not let \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* persuade you to trust in the LORD when he says, ‘The LORD will surely deliver us; this city will not be given into the hand of \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Do not listen to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, for this is what \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* says: Make peace with me \f + \fr 18:31 \ft Or Make a blessing with me\f* and come out to me. Then every one of you will eat from his own vine and his own fig tree and drink water from his own cistern, 
\v 32 until I come and take you away to a land like your own—a land of grain and new wine, a land of bread and vineyards, a land of olive trees and honey—so that you may live and not die. But do not listen to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, for he misleads you when he says, ‘The LORD will deliver us.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Has the god of any nation ever delivered his land from the hand of \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*? 
\v 34 Where are the gods of Hamath and Arpad? Where are the gods of Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivvah? Have they delivered Samaria from my hand? 
\v 35 Who among all the gods of these lands has delivered his land from my hand? How then can the LORD deliver Jerusalem from my hand?” 
\b
\m 
\v 36 But the people remained silent and did not answer a word, for \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* had commanded, “Do not answer him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 Then \x-wd Hilkiah|id="Q130270587"\x-wd*’s son \x-wd Eliakim|id="Q9252684"\x-wd* the palace administrator, \x-wd Shebna the scribe|id=""\x-wd*, and \x-wd Asaph|id="Q130243561"\x-wd*’s son \x-wd Joah|id="Q130271252"\x-wd* the recorder came to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* with their clothes torn, and they relayed to him the words of the Rabshakeh. 
\c 19
\s1 Isaiah’s Message of Deliverance
\r (Isaiah 37:1–7)
\m 
\v 1 On hearing this report, \x-wd King Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* tore his clothes, put on sackcloth, and entered the house of the LORD. 
\v 2 And he sent \x-wd Eliakim|id="Q9252684"\x-wd* the palace administrator, \x-wd Shebna the scribe|id=""\x-wd*, and the leading priests, all wearing sackcloth, to the prophet \x-wd Isaiah son of \x-wd Amoz|id="Q4748020"\x-wd*|id="Q188794"\x-wd* 
\v 3 to tell him, “This is what \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* says: Today is a day of distress, rebuke, and disgrace; for children have come to the point of birth, but there is no strength to deliver them. 
\v 4 Perhaps the LORD your God will hear all the words of the Rabshakeh, whom his master \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* has sent to defy the living God, and He will rebuke him for the words that the LORD your God has heard. Therefore lift up a prayer for the remnant that still survives.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So the servants of \x-wd King Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* went to \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd*, 
\v 6 who replied, “Tell \x-wd your master|id="Q244912"\x-wd* that this is what the LORD says: ‘Do not be afraid of the words you have heard, with which the servants of \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* have blasphemed Me. 
\v 7 Behold, I will put a spirit in him so that he will hear a rumor and return to his own land, where I will cause him to fall by the sword.’” 
\s1 Sennacherib’s Blasphemous Letter
\r (Isaiah 37:8–13)
\b
\m 
\v 8 When the Rabshakeh heard that \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* had left Lachish, he withdrew and found \x-wd the king|id="Q207140"\x-wd* fighting against Libnah. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Now \x-wd Sennacherib|id="Q207140"\x-wd* had been warned about \x-wd Tirhakah king of Cush|id=""\x-wd*:\f + \fr 19:9 \ft That is, the upper Nile region\f* “Look, he has set out to fight against you.” 
\b
\m So \x-wd Sennacherib|id="Q207140"\x-wd* again sent messengers to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, saying, 
\v 10 “Give this message to \x-wd Hezekiah king of Judah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*: 
\b
\pmo ‘Do not let your God, in whom you trust, deceive you by saying that Jerusalem will not be delivered into the hand of \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*. 
\v 11 Surely you have heard what the kings of Assyria have done to all the other countries, devoting them to destruction.\f + \fr 19:11 \ft Forms of the Hebrew cherem refer to the giving over of things or persons to the LORD, either by destroying them or by giving them as an offering.\f* Will you then be spared? 
\v 12 Did the gods of the nations destroyed by my fathers rescue those nations—the gods of Gozan, Haran, and Rezeph, and of the people of Eden in Telassar? 
\v 13 Where are the kings of Hamath, Arpad, Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivvah?’” 
\s1 Hezekiah’s Prayer
\r (Isaiah 37:14–20)
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* received the letter from the messengers, read it, and went up to the house of the LORD and spread it out before the LORD. 
\v 15 And \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* prayed before the LORD: 
\b
\pmo “O LORD, God of Israel, enthroned between the cherubim, You alone are God over all the kingdoms of the earth. You made the heavens and the earth. 
\v 16 Incline Your ear, O LORD, and hear; open Your eyes, O LORD, and see. Listen to the words that \x-wd Sennacherib|id="Q207140"\x-wd* has sent to defy the living God. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 17 Truly, O LORD, the kings of Assyria have laid waste these nations and their lands. 
\v 18 They have cast their gods into the fire and destroyed them, for they were not gods, but only wood and stone—the work of human hands. 
\b
\pmo 
\v 19 And now, O LORD our God, please save us from his hand, so that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that You alone, O LORD, are God.” 
\s1 Sennacherib’s Fall Prophesied
\r (Isaiah 37:21–35)
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \x-wd Isaiah son of \x-wd Amoz|id="Q4748020"\x-wd*|id="Q188794"\x-wd* sent a message to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*: “This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: I have heard your prayer concerning \x-wd Sennacherib king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*. 
\v 21 This is the word that the LORD has spoken against him: 
\b
\q1 ‘The Virgin Daughter of Zion 
\q2 despises you and mocks you; 
\q1 the Daughter of Jerusalem 
\q2 shakes her head behind you. 
\q1 
\v 22 Whom have you taunted and blasphemed? 
\q2 Against whom have you raised your voice 
\q1 and lifted your eyes in pride? 
\q2 Against the Holy One of Israel! 
\q1 
\v 23 Through your servants you have taunted the Lord, 
\q2 and you have said: 
\b
\q1 “With my many chariots 
\q2 I have ascended 
\q1 to the heights of the mountains, 
\q2 to the remote peaks of Lebanon. 
\q1 I have cut down its tallest cedars, 
\q2 the finest of its cypresses.\f + \fr 19:23 \ft Or pines or junipers or firs\f* 
\q1 I have reached its farthest outposts, 
\q2 the densest of its forests. 
\q1 
\v 24 I have dug wells 
\q2 and drunk foreign waters. 
\q1 With the soles of my feet 
\q2 I have dried up all the streams of Egypt.” 
\b
\q1 
\v 25 Have you not heard? 
\q2 Long ago I ordained it; 
\q1 in days of old I planned it. 
\q2 Now I have brought it to pass, 
\q1 that you should crush fortified cities 
\q2 into piles of rubble. 
\q1 
\v 26 Therefore their inhabitants, devoid of power, 
\q2 are dismayed and ashamed. 
\q1 They are like plants in the field, 
\q2 tender green shoots, 
\q1 grass on the rooftops, 
\q2 scorched before it is grown. 
\b
\q1 
\v 27 But I know your sitting down, 
\q2 your going out and coming in, 
\q2 and your raging against Me. 
\q1 
\v 28 Because your rage and arrogance against Me 
\q2 have reached My ears, 
\q1 I will put My hook in your nose 
\q2 and My bit in your mouth; 
\q1 I will send you back 
\q2 the way you came.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 29 And this will be a sign to you, O \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*: 
\b
\q1 This year you will eat 
\q2 what grows on its own, 
\q1 and in the second year 
\q2 what springs from the same. 
\q1 But in the third year you will sow and reap; 
\q2 you will plant vineyards and eat their fruit. 
\q1 
\v 30 And the surviving remnant of the house of Judah 
\q2 will again take root below 
\q2 and bear fruit above. 
\q1 
\v 31 For a remnant will go forth from Jerusalem, 
\q2 and survivors from Mount Zion. 
\q1 The zeal of the LORD of Hosts \f + \fr 19:31 \ft LXX, many Hebrew manuscripts, and an alternate MT reading; the other alternate reads The zeal of the LORD.\f* 
\q2 will accomplish this. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 So this is what the LORD says about \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*: 
\b
\q1 ‘He will not enter this city 
\q2 or shoot an arrow into it. 
\q1 He will not come before it with a shield 
\q2 or build up a siege ramp against it. 
\q1 
\v 33 He will go back the way he came, 
\q2 and he will not enter this city, 
\qr declares the LORD. 
\q1 
\v 34 I will defend this city 
\q2 and save it 
\q1 for My own sake 
\q2 and for the sake of My servant \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*.’” 
\s1 Jerusalem Delivered from the Assyrians
\r (2 Chronicles 32:20–23; Isaiah 37:36–38)
\b
\m 
\v 35 And that very night the angel of the LORD went out and struck down 185,000 men in the camp of the Assyrians. When the people got up the next morning, there were all the dead bodies! 
\v 36 So \x-wd Sennacherib king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd* broke camp and withdrew. He returned to Nineveh and stayed there. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 One day, while he was worshiping in the temple of his god Nisroch, his sons \x-wd Adrammelech|id="Q638342"\x-wd* and \x-wd Sharezer|id="Q76383242"\x-wd* \f + \fr 19:37 \ft LXX and an alternate MT reading (see also Isaiah 37:38); MT lacks his sons.\f* put him to the sword and escaped to the land of Ararat. And his son \x-wd Esar-haddon|id="Q193912"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\c 20
\s1 Hezekiah’s Illness and Recovery
\r (2 Chronicles 32:24–31; Isaiah 38:1–8)
\m 
\v 1 In those days \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* became mortally ill. The prophet \x-wd Isaiah son of \x-wd Amoz|id="Q4748020"\x-wd*|id="Q188794"\x-wd* came to him and said, “This is what the LORD says: ‘Put your house in order, for you are about to die; you will not recover.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Then \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* turned his face to the wall and prayed to the LORD, saying, 
\v 3 “Please, O LORD, remember how I have walked before You faithfully and with wholehearted devotion; I have done what is good in Your sight.” And \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* wept bitterly. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Before \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* had left the middle courtyard,\f + \fr 20:4 \ft LXX and an alternate MT reading; the other alternate reads the middle of the city\f* the word of the LORD came to him, saying, 
\v 5 “Go back and tell \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* the leader of My people that this is what the LORD, the God of your father \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*, says: ‘I have heard your prayer; I have seen your tears. I will surely heal you. On the third day from now you will go up to the house of the LORD. 
\v 6 I will add fifteen years to your life. And I will deliver you and this city from the hand of \x-wd the king of Assyria|id="Q207140"\x-wd*. I will defend this city for My sake and for the sake of My servant \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* said, “Prepare a poultice of figs.” So they brought it and applied it to the boil, and \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* recovered. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* had asked \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd*, “What will be the sign that the LORD will heal me and that I will go up to the house of the LORD on the third day?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* had replied, “This will be a sign to you from the LORD that He will do what He has promised: Would you like the shadow to go forward ten steps, or back ten steps?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “It is easy for the shadow to lengthen ten steps,” answered \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, “but not for it to go back ten steps.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So \x-wd Isaiah the prophet|id="Q188794"\x-wd* called out to the LORD, and He brought the shadow back the ten steps it had descended on the stairway of \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Hezekiah Shows His Treasures
\r (Isaiah 39:1–8)
\b
\m 
\v 12 At that time \x-wd Merodach-baladan \f + \fr 20:12 \ft Some Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, and Syriac (see also Isaiah 39:1); MT Berodach-baladan\f* son of \x-wd Baladan|id=""\x-wd*|id=""\x-wd* king of Babylon sent letters and a gift to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, for he had heard about \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*’s illness. 
\v 13 And \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* received the envoys and showed them all that was in his treasure house—the silver, the gold, the spices, and the precious oil, as well as his armory—all that was found in his storehouses. There was nothing in his palace or in all his dominion that \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* did not show them. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \x-wd the prophet Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* went to \x-wd King Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* and asked, “Where did those men come from, and what did they say to you?” 
\b
\m “They came from a distant land,” \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* replied, “from Babylon.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “What have they seen in your palace?” \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* asked. 
\b
\m “They have seen everything in my palace,” answered \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*. “There is nothing among my treasures that I did not show them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, “Hear the word of the LORD: 
\v 17 The time will surely come when everything in your palace and all that your fathers have stored up until this day will be carried off to Babylon. Nothing will be left, says the LORD. 
\v 18 And some of your descendants, your own flesh and blood, will be taken away to be eunuchs in the palace of \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Isaiah|id="Q188794"\x-wd*, “The word of the LORD that you have spoken is good.” For he thought, “Will there not at least be peace and security in my lifetime?” 
\s1 Manasseh Succeeds Hezekiah
\b
\m 
\v 20 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd*, along with all his might and how he constructed the pool and the tunnel \f + \fr 20:20 \ft Or watercourse or conduit\f* to bring water into the city, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 21 And \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* rested with his fathers, and his son \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\c 21
\s1 Manasseh Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 33:1–9)
\m 
\v 1 \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* was twelve years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem fifty-five years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Hephzibah|id="Q114771167"\x-wd*. 
\v 2 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD by following the abominations of the nations that the LORD had driven out before the Israelites. 
\v 3 For he rebuilt the high places that his father \x-wd Hezekiah|id="Q244912"\x-wd* had destroyed, and he raised up altars for Baal. He made an Asherah pole, as \x-wd King Ahab of Israel|id="Q235901"\x-wd* had done, and he worshiped and served all the host of heaven. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* also built altars in the house of the LORD, of which the LORD had said, “In Jerusalem I will put My Name.” 
\v 5 In both courtyards of the house of the LORD, he built altars to all the host of heaven. 
\v 6 He sacrificed his own son in the fire,\f + \fr 21:6 \ft Literally made his son pass through the fire\f* practiced sorcery and divination, and consulted mediums and spiritists. He did great evil in the sight of the LORD, provoking Him to anger. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* even took the carved Asherah pole he had made and set it up in the temple, of which the LORD had said to \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd* and his son \x-wd Solomon|id="Q37085"\x-wd*, “In this temple and in Jerusalem, which I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, I will establish My Name forever. 
\v 8 I will never again cause the feet of the Israelites to wander from the land that I gave to their fathers, if only they are careful to do all I have commanded them—the whole Law that My servant \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd* commanded them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But the people did not listen and \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* led them astray, so that they did greater evil than the nations that the LORD had destroyed before the Israelites. 
\s1 Manasseh’s Idolatries Rebuked
\r (2 Chronicles 33:10–20)
\b
\m 
\v 10 And the LORD spoke through His servants the prophets, saying, 
\v 11 “Since \x-wd Manasseh king of Judah|id="Q156328"\x-wd* has committed all these abominations, acting more wickedly than the Amorites who preceded him, and with his idols has caused Judah to sin, 
\v 12 this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘Behold, I am bringing such calamity upon Jerusalem and Judah that the news will reverberate in the ears of all who hear it. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 I will stretch out over Jerusalem the measuring line used against Samaria and the plumb line used against the house of \x-wd Ahab|id="Q235901"\x-wd*, and I will wipe out Jerusalem as one wipes out a bowl—wiping it and turning it upside down. 
\v 14 So I will forsake the remnant of My inheritance and deliver them into the hands of their enemies. And they will become plunder and spoil to all their enemies, 
\v 15 because they have done evil in My sight and have provoked Me to anger from the day their fathers came out of Egypt until this day.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Moreover, \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* shed so much innocent blood that he filled Jerusalem from end to end, in addition to the sin that he had caused Judah to commit, doing evil in the sight of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments and the sin that he committed, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 18 And \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* rested with his fathers and was buried in his palace garden, the garden of \x-wd Uzza|id=""\x-wd*. And his son \x-wd Amon|id="Q313423"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\s1 Amon Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 33:21–25)
\b
\m 
\v 19 \x-wd Amon|id="Q313423"\x-wd* was twenty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem two years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Meshullemeth daughter of \x-wd Haruz|id="Q115804881"\x-wd*|id="Q114995259"\x-wd*; she was from Jotbah. 
\v 20 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, as his father \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* had done. 
\v 21 He walked in all the ways of his father, and he served and worshiped the idols his father had served. 
\v 22 He abandoned the LORD, the God of his fathers, and did not walk in the way of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then the servants of \x-wd Amon|id="Q313423"\x-wd* conspired against him and killed \x-wd the king|id="Q313423"\x-wd* in his palace. 
\v 24 But the people of the land killed all those who had conspired against \x-wd King Amon|id="Q313423"\x-wd*, and they made his son \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* king in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Amon|id="Q313423"\x-wd*, along with his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\v 26 And he was buried in his tomb in the garden of \x-wd Uzza|id=""\x-wd*, and his son \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\c 22
\s1 Josiah Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 34:1–2)
\m 
\v 1 \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* was eight years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem thirty-one years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Jedidah daughter of \x-wd Adaiah|id="Q135454557"\x-wd*|id="Q135454571"\x-wd*; she was from Bozkath. 
\v 2 And he did what was right in the eyes of the LORD and walked in all the ways of his father \x-wd David|id="Q41370"\x-wd*; he did not turn aside to the right or to the left. 
\s1 Funding the Temple Repairs
\r (2 Chronicles 34:8–13)
\b
\m 
\v 3 Now in the eighteenth year of his reign, \x-wd King Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* sent the scribe, \x-wd Shaphan son of \x-wd Azaliah|id="Q9641178"\x-wd*|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Meshullam|id="Q137825058"\x-wd*, to the house of the LORD, saying, 
\v 4 “Go up to \x-wd Hilkiah the high priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd* and have him count the money that has been brought into the house of the LORD, which the doorkeepers have collected from the people. 
\v 5 And let them deliver it into the hands of the supervisors of those doing the work on the house of the LORD, who in turn are to give it to the workmen repairing the damages to the house of the LORD—
\v 6 to the carpenters, builders, and masons—to buy timber and dressed stone to repair the temple. 
\v 7 But they need not account for the money put into their hands, since they work with integrity.” 
\s1 Hilkiah Finds the Book of the Law
\r (2 Chronicles 34:14–21)
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \x-wd Hilkiah the high priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd* said to \x-wd Shaphan the scribe|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, “I have found the Book of the Law in the house of the LORD!” And he gave it to \x-wd Shaphan|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, who read it. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And \x-wd Shaphan the scribe|id="Q2904866"\x-wd* went to the king and reported, “Your servants have paid out the money that was found in the temple and have put it into the hands of the workers and supervisors of the house of the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Moreover, \x-wd Shaphan the scribe|id="Q2904866"\x-wd* told the king, “\x-wd Hilkiah the priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd* has given me a book.” And \x-wd Shaphan|id="Q2904866"\x-wd* read it in the presence of the king. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When the king heard the words of the Book of the Law, he tore his clothes 
\v 12 and commanded \x-wd Hilkiah the priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd*, \x-wd Ahikam son of \x-wd Shaphan|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q4695108"\x-wd*, \x-wd Achbor \f + \fr 22:12 \ft Achbor is another name for Abdon; see 2 Chronicles 34:20.\f* son of \x-wd Micaiah|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q123165967"\x-wd*,\f + \fr 22:12 \ft Micaiah is a variant of Micah; see 2 Chronicles 34:20.\f* \x-wd Shaphan the scribe|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, and \x-wd Asaiah the servant of the king|id="Q4803405"\x-wd*: 
\v 13 “Go and inquire of the LORD for me, for the people, and for all Judah concerning the words in this book that has been found. For great is the wrath of the LORD that burns against us because our fathers have not obeyed the words of this book by doing all that is written about us.” 
\s1 Huldah’s Prophecy
\r (2 Chronicles 34:22–28)
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \x-wd Hilkiah the priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd*, \x-wd Ahikam|id="Q4695108"\x-wd*, \x-wd Achbor|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, \x-wd Shaphan|id="Q2904866"\x-wd*, and \x-wd Asaiah|id="Q4803405"\x-wd* went and spoke to \x-wd Huldah the prophetess|id="Q583207"\x-wd*, the wife of \x-wd Shallum son of \x-wd Tikvah|id=""\x-wd*|id=""\x-wd*,\f + \fr 22:14 \ft Tikvah is a variant of Tokhath; see 2 Chronicles 34:22.\f* the son of \x-wd Harhas|id=""\x-wd*,\f + \fr 22:14 \ft Harhas is a variant of Hasrah; see 2 Chronicles 34:22.\f* the keeper of the wardrobe. She lived in Jerusalem, in the Second District.\f + \fr 22:14 \ft Or the Second Quarter, a newer section of Jerusalem; Hebrew the Mishneh\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And \x-wd Huldah|id="Q583207"\x-wd* said to them, “This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘Tell \x-wd the man who sent you|id="Q313228"\x-wd* 
\v 16 that this is what the LORD says: I am about to bring calamity on this place and on its people, according to all the words of the book that the king of Judah has read, 
\v 17 because they have forsaken Me and burned incense to other gods, that they might provoke Me to anger with all the works of their hands. My wrath will be kindled against this place and will not be quenched.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 18 But as for \x-wd the king of Judah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*, who sent you to inquire of the LORD, tell him that this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘As for the words that you heard, 
\v 19 because your heart was tender and you humbled yourself before the LORD when you heard what I spoke against this place and against its people, that they would become a desolation and a curse, and because you have torn your clothes and wept before Me, I have heard you,’ declares the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 ‘Therefore I will indeed gather you to your fathers, and you will be gathered to your grave in peace. Your eyes will not see all the calamity that I will bring on this place.’” 
\b
\m So they brought her answer back to \x-wd the king|id="Q313228"\x-wd*. 
\c 23
\s1 Josiah Renews the Covenant
\r (2 Chronicles 34:29–33)
\m 
\v 1 Then \x-wd the king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* summoned all the elders of Judah and Jerusalem. 
\v 2 And he went up to the house of the LORD with all the people of Judah and Jerusalem, as well as the priests and the prophets—all the people small and great—and in their hearing he read all the words of the Book of the Covenant that had been found in the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \x-wd the king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* stood by the pillar and made a covenant before the LORD to follow the LORD and to keep His commandments, decrees, and statutes with all his heart and all his soul, and to carry out the words of the covenant that were written in this book. And all the people entered into the covenant. 
\s1 Josiah Destroys Idolatry
\r (1 Kings 13:1–10; 2 Chronicles 34:3–7)
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \x-wd the king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* commanded \x-wd Hilkiah the high priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd*, the priests second in rank, and the doorkeepers to remove from the temple of the LORD all the articles made for Baal, Asherah, and all the host of heaven. And he burned them outside Jerusalem in the fields of Kidron and carried their ashes to Bethel. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* also did away with the idolatrous priests ordained by the kings of Judah to burn incense on the high places of the cities of Judah and in the places all around Jerusalem—those who had burned incense to Baal, to the sun and moon, to the constellations, and to all the host of heaven. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 He brought the Asherah pole from the house of the LORD to the Kidron Valley outside Jerusalem, and there he burned it, ground it to powder, and threw its dust on the graves of the common people. 
\v 7 He also tore down the quarters of the male shrine prostitutes that were in the house of the LORD, where the women had woven tapestries for Asherah. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* brought all the priests from the cities of Judah and desecrated the high places, from Geba to Beersheba, where the priests had burned incense. He tore down the high places of the gates at the entrance of the gate of \x-wd Joshua the governor of the city|id=""\x-wd*, which was to the left of the city gate. 
\v 9 Although the priests of the high places did not come up to the altar of the LORD in Jerusalem, they ate unleavened bread with their fellow priests. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 He also desecrated Topheth in the Valley of Ben-hinnom \f + \fr 23:10 \ft Or the Valley of the Son of Hinnom\f* so that no one could sacrifice his son or daughter in the fire \f + \fr 23:10 \ft Literally could pass his son or daughter through the fire\f* to Molech. 
\v 11 And he removed from the entrance to the house of the LORD the horses that the kings of Judah had dedicated to the sun. They were in the court \f + \fr 23:11 \ft Hebrew parbarim\f* near the chamber of an official named \x-wd Nathan-melech|id="Q6968837"\x-wd*. And \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* burned up the chariots of the sun. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 He pulled down the altars that the kings of Judah had set up on the roof near the upper chamber of \x-wd Ahaz|id="Q30914"\x-wd*, and the altars that \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* had set up in the two courtyards of the house of the LORD. \x-wd The king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* pulverized them there \f + \fr 23:12 \ft Or quickly removed them from there\f* and threw their dust into the Kidron Valley. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \x-wd The king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* also desecrated the high places east of Jerusalem, to the south of the Mount of Corruption, which \x-wd King Solomon of Israel|id="Q37085"\x-wd* had built for Ashtoreth the abomination of the Sidonians, for Chemosh the abomination of the Moabites, and for Milcom \f + \fr 23:13 \ft Milcom is a variant of Molech; see Leviticus 18:21 and 1 Kings 11:7.\f* the abomination of the Ammonites. 
\v 14 He smashed the sacred pillars to pieces, cut down the Asherah poles, and covered the sites with human bones. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 He even pulled down the altar at Bethel, the high place set up by \x-wd Jeroboam son of \x-wd Nebat|id="Q26835883"\x-wd*|id="Q313219"\x-wd*, who had caused Israel to sin. Then he burned \f + \fr 23:15 \ft Hebrew; LXX broke into pieces\f* the high place, ground it to powder, and burned the Asherah pole. 
\v 16 And as \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* turned, he saw the tombs there on the hillside, and he sent someone to take the bones out of the tombs, and he burned them on the altar to defile it, according to the word of the LORD proclaimed by \x-wd the man of God|id=""\x-wd* who had foretold these things.\f + \fr 23:16 \ft See 1 Kings 13:2; Hebrew; LXX includes when Jeroboam stood by the altar at the feast. And he turned and lifted his eyes to the tomb of the man of God.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \x-wd the king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* asked, “What is this monument I see?” 
\b
\m And the men of the city replied, “It is the tomb of \x-wd the man of God|id=""\x-wd* who came from Judah and proclaimed these things that you have done to the altar of Bethel.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “Let him rest,” said \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*. “Do not let anyone disturb his bones.” 
\b
\m So they left his bones undisturbed, along with those of \x-wd the prophet|id=""\x-wd* who had come from Samaria. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Just as \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* had done at Bethel, so also in the cities of Samaria he removed all the shrines of the high places set up by the kings of Israel who had provoked the LORD to anger. 
\v 20 On the altars he slaughtered all the priests of the high places, and he burned human bones on them. Then he returned to Jerusalem. 
\s1 Josiah Restores the Passover
\r (2 Chronicles 35:1–19)
\b
\m 
\v 21 \x-wd The king|id="Q313228"\x-wd* commanded all the people, “Keep the Passover of the LORD your God, as it is written in this Book of the Covenant.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 No such Passover had been observed from the days of the judges who had governed Israel through all the days of the kings of Israel and Judah. 
\v 23 But in the eighteenth year of \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*’s reign, this Passover was observed to the LORD in Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Furthermore, \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* removed the mediums and spiritists, the household gods and idols, and all the abominations that were seen in the land of Judah and in Jerusalem. He did this to carry out the words of the law written in the book that \x-wd Hilkiah the priest|id="Q3276278"\x-wd* had found in the house of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Neither before nor after \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* was there any king like him, who turned to the LORD with all his heart and with all his soul and with all his strength, according to all the Law of \x-wd Moses|id="Q9077"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Nevertheless, the LORD did not turn away from the fury of His burning anger, which was kindled against Judah because of all that \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* had done to provoke Him to anger. 
\v 27 For the LORD had said, “I will remove Judah from My sight, just as I removed Israel. I will reject this city Jerusalem, which I chose, and the temple of which I said, ‘My Name shall be there.’” 
\s1 The Death of Josiah
\r (2 Chronicles 35:20–24)
\b
\m 
\v 28 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* and all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\b
\m 
\v 29 During \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*’s reign, \x-wd Pharaoh Neco king of Egypt|id=""\x-wd* marched up to help \x-wd the king of Assyria|id=""\x-wd* at the Euphrates River. \x-wd King Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd* went out to confront him, but \x-wd Neco|id=""\x-wd* faced him and killed him at Megiddo. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 From Megiddo his servants carried his body in a chariot, brought him to Jerusalem, and buried him in his own tomb. Then the people of the land took \x-wd Jehoahaz son of \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*|id="Q336832"\x-wd*, anointed him, and made him king in place of his father. 
\s1 Jehoahaz Succeeds Josiah
\r (2 Chronicles 36:1–4)
\b
\m 
\v 31 \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q336832"\x-wd* was twenty-three years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem three months. His mother’s name was \x-wd Hamutal daughter of \x-wd Jeremiah|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q16573951"\x-wd*; she was from Libnah. 
\v 32 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, just as his fathers had done. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 And \x-wd Pharaoh Neco|id=""\x-wd* imprisoned \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q336832"\x-wd* at Riblah in the land of Hamath so that he could not reign in Jerusalem, and he imposed on Judah a levy of a hundred talents of silver \f + \fr 23:33 \ft 100 talents is approximately 3.77 tons or 3.42 metric tons of silver.\f* and a talent of gold.\f + \fr 23:33 \ft A talent is approximately 75.4 pounds or 34.2 kilograms of gold.\f* 
\v 34 Then \x-wd Pharaoh Neco|id=""\x-wd* made \x-wd Eliakim son of \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*|id="Q319034"\x-wd* king in place of his father \x-wd Josiah|id="Q313228"\x-wd*, and he changed \x-wd Eliakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd*’s name to \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd*. But \x-wd Neco|id=""\x-wd* took \x-wd Jehoahaz|id="Q336832"\x-wd* and carried him off to Egypt, where he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 So \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* paid the silver and gold to \x-wd Pharaoh Neco|id=""\x-wd*, but to meet \x-wd Pharaoh|id=""\x-wd*’s demand he taxed the land and exacted the silver and the gold from the people, each according to his wealth. 
\s1 Jehoiakim Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 36:5–8)
\b
\m 
\v 36 \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* was twenty-five years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eleven years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Zebidah daughter of \x-wd Pedaiah|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q30527376"\x-wd*; she was from Rumah. 
\v 37 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, just as his fathers had done. 
\c 24
\s1 Babylon Controls Jehoiakim
\m 
\v 1 During \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd*’s reign, \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* invaded. So \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* became his vassal for three years, until he turned and rebelled against \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar|id="Q12591"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And the LORD sent Chaldean,\f + \fr 24:2 \ft Or Babylonian\f* Aramean, Moabite, and Ammonite raiders against \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* in order to destroy Judah, according to the word that the LORD had spoken through His servants the prophets. 
\v 3 Surely this happened to Judah at the LORD’s command, to remove them from His presence because of the sins of \x-wd Manasseh|id="Q156328"\x-wd* and all that he had done, 
\v 4 and also for the innocent blood he had shed. For he had filled Jerusalem with innocent blood, and the LORD was unwilling to forgive. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 As for the rest of the acts of \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd*, along with all his accomplishments, are they not written in the Book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Judah? 
\s1 Jehoiachin Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 36:9–10)
\b
\m 
\v 6 And \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* rested with his fathers, and his son \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* reigned in his place. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now the \x-wd the king of Egypt|id=""\x-wd* did not march out of his land again, because \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* had taken all his territory, from the Brook of Egypt to the Euphrates River. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* was eighteen years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem three months. His mother’s name was \x-wd Nehushta daughter of \x-wd Elnathan|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q20502297"\x-wd*; she was from Jerusalem. 
\v 9 And he did evil in the sight of the LORD, just as his father had done. 
\s1 The Captivity of Jerusalem
\r (Lamentations 1:1–22)
\b
\m 
\v 10 At that time the servants of \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* marched up to Jerusalem, and the city came under siege. 
\v 11 And \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* came to the city while his servants were besieging it. 
\v 12 \x-wd Jehoiachin king of Judah|id="Q319049"\x-wd*, his mother, his servants, his commanders, and his officials all surrendered to \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd*. 
\b
\m So in the eighth year of his reign, \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* took him captive. 
\v 13 As the LORD had declared, \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar|id="Q12591"\x-wd* also carried off all the treasures from the house of the LORD and the royal palace, and he cut into pieces all the gold articles that \x-wd Solomon king of Israel|id="Q37085"\x-wd* had made in the temple of the LORD. 
\v 14 He carried into exile all Jerusalem—all the commanders and mighty men of valor, all the craftsmen and metalsmiths—ten thousand captives in all. Only the poorest people of the land remained. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar|id="Q12591"\x-wd* carried away \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* to Babylon, as well as \x-wd \x-wd the king|id="Q319049"\x-wd*’s mother|id="Q20502297"\x-wd*, his wives, his officials, and the leading men of the land. He took them into exile from Jerusalem to Babylon. 
\v 16 \x-wd The king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* also brought into exile to Babylon all seven thousand men of valor and a thousand craftsmen and metalsmiths—all strong and fit for battle. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* made \x-wd Mattaniah|id="Q184273"\x-wd*, \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd*’s uncle, king in his place and changed his name to \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd*. 
\s1 Zedekiah Reigns in Judah
\r (2 Chronicles 36:11–14; Jeremiah 52:1–3)
\b
\m 
\v 18 \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd* was twenty-one years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eleven years. His mother’s name was \x-wd Hamutal daughter of \x-wd Jeremiah|id=""\x-wd*|id="Q16573951"\x-wd*; she was from Libnah. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd* did evil in the sight of the LORD, just as \x-wd Jehoiakim|id="Q319034"\x-wd* had done. 
\v 20 For because of the anger of the LORD, all this happened in Jerusalem and Judah, until He finally banished them from His presence. 
\b
\m And \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd* also rebelled against \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd*. 
\c 25
\s1 Nebuchadnezzar Besieges Jerusalem
\r (2 Chronicles 36:15–21; Jeremiah 39:1–10)
\m 
\v 1 So in the ninth year of \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd*’s reign, on the tenth day of the tenth month, \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* marched against Jerusalem with his entire army. They encamped outside the city and built \f + \fr 25:1 \ft Literally He encamped outside it and they built\f* a siege wall all around it. 
\v 2 And the city was kept under siege until \x-wd King Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd*’s eleventh year. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 By the ninth day of the fourth month,\f + \fr 25:3 \ft Probable reading (see Jeremiah 52:6); MT does not include fourth.\f* the famine in the city was so severe that the people of the land had no food. 
\v 4 Then the city was breached; and though the Chaldeans \f + \fr 25:4 \ft That is, the Babylonians; also in verses 5, 6, 10, 13, 24, 25, and 26 \f* had surrounded the city, all the men of war fled by night by way of the gate between the two walls near the king’s garden. 
\b
\m They headed toward the Arabah,\f + \fr 25:4 \ft Or the Jordan Valley\f* 
\v 5 but the army of the Chaldeans pursued \x-wd the king|id="Q184273"\x-wd* and overtook him in the plains of Jericho, and his whole army deserted him. 
\v 6 The Chaldeans seized \x-wd the king|id="Q184273"\x-wd* and brought him up to \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* at Riblah, where they pronounced judgment on him. 
\v 7 And they slaughtered the sons of \x-wd Zedekiah|id="Q184273"\x-wd* before his eyes. Then they put out his eyes, bound him with bronze shackles, and took him to Babylon. 
\s1 The Temple Destroyed
\r (Jeremiah 52:12–23)
\b
\m 
\v 8 On the seventh day of the fifth month, in the nineteenth year of \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar|id="Q12591"\x-wd*’s reign over Babylon, \x-wd Nebuzaradan captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd*, a servant of \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd*, entered Jerusalem. 
\v 9 He burned down the house of the LORD, the royal palace, and all the houses of Jerusalem—every significant building. 
\v 10 And the whole army of the Chaldeans under \x-wd the captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* broke down the walls around Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \x-wd Nebuzaradan captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* carried into exile the people who remained in the city, along with the deserters who had defected to \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* and the rest of the population. 
\v 12 But \x-wd the captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* left behind some of the poorest of the land to tend the vineyards and fields. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Moreover, the Chaldeans broke up the bronze pillars and stands and the bronze Sea in the house of the LORD, and they carried the bronze to Babylon. 
\v 14 They also took away the pots, shovels, wick trimmers, dishes, and all the articles of bronze used in the temple service. 
\v 15 \x-wd The captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* also took away the censers and sprinkling bowls—anything made of pure gold or fine silver. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 As for the two pillars, the Sea, and the movable stands that \x-wd Solomon|id="Q37085"\x-wd* had made for the house of the LORD, the weight of the bronze from all these articles was beyond measure. 
\v 17 Each pillar was eighteen cubits tall.\f + \fr 25:17 \ft 18 cubits is approximately 27 feet or 8.2 meters.\f* The bronze capital atop one pillar was three cubits high,\f + \fr 25:17 \ft 3 cubits is approximately 4.5 feet or 1.4 meters.\f* with a network of bronze pomegranates all around. The second pillar, with its network, was similar. 
\s1 Captives Carried to Babylon
\r (Jeremiah 52:24–30)
\b
\m 
\v 18 \x-wd The captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* also took away \x-wd Seraiah the chief priest|id="Q18192237"\x-wd*, \x-wd Zephaniah the priest of second rank|id=""\x-wd*, and \x-wd the three doorkeepers|id=""\x-wd*. 
\v 19 Of those still in the city, he took \x-wd a court official|id=""\x-wd* who had been appointed over the men of war, as well as \x-wd five royal advisors|id=""\x-wd*. He also took \x-wd the scribe of the captain of the army|id=""\x-wd*, who had enlisted the people of the land, and sixty men who were found in the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 \x-wd Nebuzaradan captain of the guard|id="Q6903514"\x-wd* took them and brought them to \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* at Riblah. 
\v 21 There at Riblah in the land of Hamath, \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* struck them down and put them to death. So Judah was taken into exile, away from its own land. 
\s1 Gedaliah Governs in Judah
\r (Jeremiah 40:1–16)
\b
\m 
\v 22 \x-wd Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* appointed \x-wd Gedaliah son of \x-wd Ahikam|id="Q4695108"\x-wd*|id="Q1497453"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Shaphan|id=""\x-wd*, over the people he had left behind in the land of Judah. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 When all the commanders of the armies and their men heard that \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd* had appointed \x-wd Gedaliah|id="Q1497453"\x-wd* as governor, they came to \x-wd Gedaliah|id="Q1497453"\x-wd* at Mizpah—\x-wd Ishmael son of \x-wd Nethaniah|id="Q124344879"\x-wd*|id="Q2903835"\x-wd*, \x-wd Johanan son of \x-wd Kareah|id=""\x-wd*|id=""\x-wd*, \x-wd Seraiah son of \x-wd Tanhumeth the Netophathite|id=""\x-wd*|id=""\x-wd*, and \x-wd Jaazaniah \f + \fr 25:23 \ft Jaazaniah is a variant of Jezaniah; see Jeremiah 40:8.\f* son of the Maacathite|id=""\x-wd*, as well as their men. 
\v 24 And \x-wd Gedaliah|id="Q1497453"\x-wd* took an oath before them and their men, assuring them, “Do not be afraid of the servants of the Chaldeans. Live in the land and serve \x-wd the king of Babylon|id="Q12591"\x-wd*, and it will be well with you.” 
\s1 The Murder of Gedaliah
\r (Jeremiah 41:1–10)
\b
\m 
\v 25 In the seventh month, however, \x-wd Ishmael son of \x-wd Nethaniah|id="Q124344879"\x-wd*|id="Q2903835"\x-wd*, the son of \x-wd Elishama|id="Q124310229"\x-wd*, who was a member of the royal family, came with ten men and struck down and killed \x-wd Gedaliah|id="Q1497453"\x-wd*, along with the Judeans and Chaldeans who were with him at Mizpah. 
\v 26 Then all the people small and great, together with the commanders of the army, arose and fled to Egypt for fear of the Chaldeans. 
\s1 Jehoiachin Released from Prison
\r (Jeremiah 52:31–34)
\b
\m 
\v 27 On the twenty-seventh day of the twelfth month of the thirty-seventh year of the exile of Judah’s \x-wd King Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd*, in the year \x-wd Evil-merodach|id="Q313234"\x-wd* became king of Babylon, he released \f + \fr 25:27 \ft Literally lifted up the head of\f* \x-wd King Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* of Judah from prison. 
\v 28 And he spoke kindly to \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* and set his throne above the thrones of the other kings who were with him in Babylon. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* changed out of his prison clothes, and he dined regularly at \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd*’s table for the rest of his life. 
\v 30 And \x-wd the king|id=""\x-wd* provided \x-wd Jehoiachin|id="Q319049"\x-wd* a daily portion for the rest of his life. 
